/* Periodic Coast - past-exam twins, set A (CHM 11510).
   Original questions modelled on the skills tested in past Purdue CHM 115 exams (Boilerexams
   practice sets: 2017 Fall Final, 2018 Fall Final, 2021 Fall Exam 1 practice), written fresh -
   no exam text is reproduced. Each record's src names the exam item it twins so coverage can be
   audited; items the old exams' students got wrong most often (accuracy below 60%) have two
   twins, the second slightly harder, and the Evening Exam 2 topics (gyms 5-7: thermochemistry,
   light and quantum theory, configurations and periodicity) get extra twins.
   Skipped as outside the Fall 2026 course: organic functional groups and isomers, IR
   spectroscopy, Beer's law, synthetic polymers, and orbital hybridization / sigma-pi counting
   (Silberberg Ch 11 is not on the schedule).
   Every numeric answer and every numeric distractor was recomputed with a Node script from the
   course constants (h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s, c = 2.998 × 10⁸ m/s, N_A = 6.022 × 10²³,
   Bohr 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J, R = 8.314 J/(mol·K)) and the course atomic masses. */
chemAddQuestions([
/* ---------- Gym 1: measurement ---------- */
["m1-p-001",1,1,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "A student multiplies two measured values on a calculator, 0.04250 × 31.6, and the display reads 1.343. How should the product be reported?",
 ["1.343","1.3","1.34","1.3430","1.34300"],2,
 "Multiplication keeps the fewest significant figures of the inputs. 0.04250 has 4 (leading zeros never count; the trailing zero after the decimal point does) and 31.6 has 3, so the answer gets 3: 1.34. 1.343 and 1.3430 keep calculator digits (4 and 5 sig figs) and 1.34300 pads even more. 1.3 applies the addition rule (fewest decimal places, as in 31.6) to a multiplication.",
 ["Multiplying or dividing: count significant figures, not decimal places.","Count the sig figs in 0.04250 (leading zeros don't count) and in 31.6.","The smaller count is 3."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q1"}],
["m1-p-002",1,1,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "A handbook lists copper at 8960 kg/m³. Rewrite that value in g/cm³. (1 m³ = 10⁶ cm³)",
 ["8.96 × 10⁶ g/cm³","0.00896 g/cm³","8.96 × 10⁴ g/cm³","8.96 g/cm³","8.96 × 10¹² g/cm³"],3,
 "Convert both units: 8960 kg/m³ × (1000 g / 1 kg) × (1 m³ / 10⁶ cm³) = 8.96 g/cm³. 0.00896 forgets kg → g; 8.96 × 10⁶ converts kg → g but leaves the volume in m³; 8.96 × 10⁴ divides by 100 (cm per m) without cubing it; 8.96 × 10¹² multiplies by 10⁶ instead of dividing.",
 ["Two conversions are needed: mass (kg → g) and volume (m³ → cm³).","Multiply by 1000 g/kg and by (1 m³ / 10⁶ cm³) so the units cancel.","8960 × 1000 ÷ 10⁶."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q2"}],
["m1-p-003",1,1,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "A 2.50-carat ruby is set in a ring. Ruby's density is 4.00 g/cm³, and the carat is a mass unit equal to 0.200 g. What volume does the ruby occupy?",
 ["0.625 cm³","0.125 cm³","2.00 cm³","3.13 cm³","8.00 cm³"],1,
 "Mass first: 2.50 carats × 0.200 g/carat = 0.500 g. Then V = m/d = 0.500 g ÷ 4.00 g/cm³ = 0.125 cm³. 0.625 cm³ divides carats by density (skips the carat → gram step); 3.13 cm³ divides 2.50 by 0.200 (the conversion turned upside down, 12.5 g); 2.00 cm³ multiplies mass by density; 8.00 cm³ divides density by mass.",
 ["Density is in g/cm³, so the mass must be in grams first.","grams = carats × 0.200; then volume = mass ÷ density.","0.500 g ÷ 4.00 g/cm³."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q3"}],

/* ---------- Gym 2: atoms, ions and names ---------- */
["m2-p-001",2,2,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "An oxygen-18 atom contains how many neutrons?",
 ["8","18","10","26","9"],2,
 "Oxygen's atomic number is 8, so it has 8 protons. The 18 is the mass number A = protons + neutrons, so N = 18 − 8 = 10. 8 is the proton count; 18 is the mass number itself; 26 adds A and Z; 9 just halves the mass number.",
 ["The number after the element's name is the mass number.","Neutrons = mass number − atomic number.","Oxygen is element 8."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q4"}],
["m2-p-002",2,2,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Two of the four nuclides ⁵⁶₂₆Fe, ⁵⁸₂₆Fe, ⁵⁸₂₈Ni and ⁶⁰₂₇Co have equal neutron counts. Which two?",
 ["⁵⁶₂₆Fe and ⁵⁸₂₆Fe","⁵⁸₂₆Fe and ⁵⁸₂₈Ni","⁵⁸₂₈Ni and ⁶⁰₂₇Co","⁵⁶₂₆Fe and ⁶⁰₂₇Co","⁵⁶₂₆Fe and ⁵⁸₂₈Ni"],4,
 "N = A − Z: ⁵⁶Fe 56 − 26 = 30; ⁵⁸Fe 58 − 26 = 32; ⁵⁸Ni 58 − 28 = 30; ⁶⁰Co 60 − 27 = 33. Only ⁵⁶Fe and ⁵⁸Ni share 30 neutrons. The two iron atoms share protons (they are isotopes), and ⁵⁸Fe and ⁵⁸Ni share a mass number - neither means equal neutrons.",
 ["Work out N = A − Z for each nuclide.","Same element means same protons; same top number means same A. Neither means same N.","30, 32, 30, 33."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q5"}],
["m2-p-003",2,2,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Two neutral atoms are isotopes of the same element. Which quantity MUST be different for the two atoms?",
 ["Atomic number","Mass number","Number of protons","Number of electrons","Chemical symbol"],1,
 "Isotopes have the same number of protons (same Z, same symbol, and as neutral atoms the same number of electrons) but different numbers of neutrons. Because A = Z + N, a different N forces a different mass number. Every other choice is something two isotopes share.",
 ["What decides which element an atom is?","Isotopes differ only in their neutron count.","If N changes and Z does not, what happens to Z + N?"],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q6"}],
["m2-p-004",2,2,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "A particle contains 35 protons, 44 neutrons and 36 electrons. What is it?",
 ["Kr","Br⁺","Se⁻","Br⁻","Rb⁺"],3,
 "35 protons makes it bromine (Z = 35) whatever the electrons do. One more electron than protons gives a charge of 35 − 36 = −1: Br⁻ (specifically ⁷⁹Br⁻, since 35 + 44 = 79). Kr has 36 electrons but 36 protons; Rb⁺ has 36 electrons but 37 protons; Br⁺ would have only 34 electrons; Se⁻ has 34 protons. Identify the element by its protons, never by its electrons.",
 ["The proton count alone names the element.","Charge = protons − electrons.","Element 35 with one extra electron."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q7"}],
["m2-p-005",2,3,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "What is the formula of aluminum sulfide?",
 ["Al₂S₃","AlS","Al₃S₂","AlS₃","Al₂S"],0,
 "Al forms Al³⁺ and S forms S²⁻. Balance the charges: 2(+3) + 3(−2) = 0, so Al₂S₃. AlS ignores the different charges; Al₃S₂ swaps the subscripts (+9 against −4); AlS₃ (+3 against −6) and Al₂S (+6 against −2) do not balance either.",
 ["Find each ion's charge from its group.","Al is in group 13 and S in group 16.","Use the lowest whole numbers that make (+3)x + (−2)y = 0."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q8"}],
["m2-p-006",2,2,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "A neutral sulfur atom gains two electrons. The particle it becomes is",
 ["a cation with a 2+ charge","an anion with a 2+ charge","an anion with a 2− charge","an isotope of sulfur with a mass number 2 higher","a cation with a 2− charge"],2,
 "Electrons are negative, so gaining two of them gives a net 2− charge, and negative ions are anions: S²⁻. Electrons have almost no mass and do not change the mass number, so this is not a new isotope. Cations are positive ions, formed by LOSING electrons.",
 ["Which particle carries a negative charge?","Gaining negative charge makes the particle negative.","A negative ion is an anion."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q9"}],
["m2-p-007",2,3,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Magnesium burns in nitrogen gas to give magnesium nitride. Identify its formula and the kind of bonding that holds it together.",
 ["MgN, ionic","Mg₃N₂, covalent","Mg₂N₃, ionic","Mg₃N₂, ionic","MgN₂, covalent"],3,
 "Mg (group 2) forms Mg²⁺ and N (group 15) forms N³⁻. Charge balance: 3(+2) + 2(−3) = 0 → Mg₃N₂. A metal combined with a nonmetal gives an ionic compound. MgN ignores the charges; Mg₂N₃ swaps the subscripts; the covalent choices miss that a metal-nonmetal compound is ionic.",
 ["Metal + nonmetal → which type of bonding?","The ions are Mg²⁺ and N³⁻.","Cross the charges: 3 Mg for every 2 N."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q10"}],
["m2-p-008",2,3,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Water-treatment plants add aluminum sulfate to make suspended particles settle. Which formula is correct for this compound?",
 ["AlSO₄","Al₃(SO₄)₂","Al₂SO₄₃","Al(SO₄)₃","Al₂(SO₄)₃"],4,
 "Al³⁺ combines with the polyatomic sulfate ion SO₄²⁻. Two Al³⁺ (+6) balance three SO₄²⁻ (−6): Al₂(SO₄)₃. Parentheses are required when more than one polyatomic ion is present - Al₂SO₄₃ would mean 43 oxygen atoms. AlSO₄ ignores the charges; Al₃(SO₄)₂ swaps the subscripts; Al(SO₄)₃ would carry an overall charge of 3−.",
 ["Sulfate is SO₄²⁻ and stays together as a unit.","Al is +3. Balance (+3)x with (−2)y.","Two Al and three sulfates, with the sulfate in parentheses."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q11"}],
["m2-p-009",2,2,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "How many protons, neutrons and electrons are in the ion ⁵⁶Fe³⁺?",
 ["26 protons, 30 neutrons, 23 electrons","26 protons, 56 neutrons, 23 electrons","26 protons, 30 neutrons, 29 electrons","30 protons, 26 neutrons, 23 electrons","56 protons, 26 neutrons, 23 electrons"],0,
 "Iron is element 26: 26 protons. Neutrons = 56 − 26 = 30. A 3+ charge means three fewer electrons than protons: 26 − 3 = 23. 29 electrons ADDS three (that would be a 3− ion); 56 neutrons uses the mass number as the neutron count; the last two mix up the roles of Z, N and A.",
 ["Z for iron comes from the periodic table.","N = A − Z; a positive charge means electrons were lost.","26 − 3 electrons."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q15"}],

/* ---------- Gym 3: the mole and stoichiometry ---------- */
["m3-p-001",3,3,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Potassium chlorate (KClO₃) releases oxygen in emergency oxygen generators. What is the mass of 3.25 mol of KClO₃?",
 ["294 g","398 g","242 g","37.7 g","0.0265 g"],1,
 "Molar mass of KClO₃ = 39.10 + 35.45 + 3(16.00) = 122.55 g/mol. Mass = 3.25 mol × 122.55 g/mol = 398 g. 294 g counts only one O (KClO, 90.55 g/mol); 242 g leaves the oxygen out entirely (KCl); 37.7 g divides the molar mass by the moles; 0.0265 g divides the moles by the molar mass.",
 ["moles → grams: multiply by the molar mass.","Add K + Cl + three O.","3.25 × 122.55."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q14"}],
["m3-p-002",3,3,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Rank by oxygen content: which sample holds the most O atoms?",
 ["4.0 g of O₂","0.40 mol of H₂O","2.0 × 10²³ molecules of CO₂","0.10 mol of P₄O₁₀","50.0 mL of 2.00 M H₂SO₄"],3,
 "Put every sample in moles of O atoms. O₂: 4.0 g ÷ 32.00 g/mol = 0.125 mol × 2 = 0.25 mol O. H₂O: 0.40 × 1 = 0.40 mol O. CO₂: 2.0 × 10²³ ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 0.332 mol × 2 = 0.66 mol O. P₄O₁₀: 0.10 × 10 = 1.0 mol O. H₂SO₄: 0.0500 L × 2.00 M = 0.100 mol × 4 = 0.40 mol O. The smallest-looking amount, 0.10 mol, wins because every molecule carries ten O atoms.",
 ["Convert each sample to moles of molecules first.","Then multiply by the number of O atoms in one formula unit.","For the solution, moles = M × V(L)."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q12"}],
["m3-p-003",3,3,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which sample contains the most oxygen atoms?",
 ["4.0 × 10²³ molecules of O₃","25.0 g of H₂O","25.0 g of O₂","25.0 g of CO₂","100.0 mL of 3.00 M H₃PO₄"],0,
 "Moles of O atoms: O₃: 4.0 × 10²³ ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 0.664 mol × 3 = 1.99 mol O. O₂: 25.0 ÷ 32.00 = 0.781 mol × 2 = 1.56 mol O. H₂O: 25.0 ÷ 18.02 = 1.39 mol O. CO₂: 25.0 ÷ 44.01 = 0.568 mol × 2 = 1.14 mol O. H₃PO₄: 0.1000 L × 3.00 M = 0.300 mol × 4 = 1.20 mol O. Among the equal masses, O₂ has the most O because all of its mass is oxygen - but the ozone sample beats them all. Comparing raw grams or raw molecule counts without converting is the trap.",
 ["Every sample must become 'moles of O atoms' before you compare.","Molecules ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = moles; grams ÷ molar mass = moles; M × L = moles.","Multiply by the O subscript: 3 for O₃, 4 for H₃PO₄."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q12"}],
["m3-p-004",3,3,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Three samples: (a) 1.0 mol of C₂H₆, (b) 2.0 mol of NH₃, (c) 3.0 mol of CO₂. Which has the largest total count of atoms?",
 ["(a), because ethane has the most atoms per molecule","(b)","(c)","All three have equal numbers of atoms","(a) and (b) tie for the most"],2,
 "Multiply moles of molecules by atoms per molecule: C₂H₆ 1.0 × 8 = 8.0 mol of atoms; NH₃ 2.0 × 4 = 8.0 mol; CO₂ 3.0 × 3 = 9.0 mol. (c) wins. (a) and (b) do tie, but at 8.0 mol, below (c). Having the most atoms per molecule is not the same as having the most atoms in the sample.",
 ["Total atoms = moles of molecules × atoms in one molecule.","Count: C₂H₆ has 8 atoms, NH₃ has 4, CO₂ has 3.","Compare 8.0, 8.0 and 9.0 mol of atoms."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q13"}],
["m3-p-005",3,3,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Each sample below is weighed out on a balance. Which holds the largest number of atoms altogether?",
 ["32.00 g of O₂","18.02 g of H₂O","17.03 g of NH₃","16.04 g of CH₄","All four have equal numbers of atoms, since each is one mole"],3,
 "Each sample is 1.000 mol of molecules (its mass equals its molar mass), so compare atoms per molecule: O₂ 2, H₂O 3, NH₃ 4, CH₄ 5. CH₄ has 5.00 mol of atoms (3.01 × 10²⁴ atoms). One mole of molecules is the same NUMBER OF MOLECULES, not the same number of atoms - that is the trap in the last choice.",
 ["Find the moles in each sample first - notice anything about the masses?","Equal moles of molecules does not mean equal numbers of atoms.","Count the atoms in one molecule of each."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q13"}],
["m3-p-006",3,4,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "1-Propanol burns completely in oxygen: C₃H₈O(l) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + H₂O(g) (unbalanced). What amount of O₂, in moles, does the complete combustion of 9.20 g of 1-propanol use up?",
 ["0.153 mol","0.306 mol","0.765 mol","1.38 mol","0.689 mol"],4,
 "Balance C first (3 CO₂), then H (4 H₂O), then O: the products hold 3(2) + 4 = 10 O, propanol supplies 1, so O₂ must supply 9 O = 4.5 O₂. Doubled: 2C₃H₈O + 9O₂ → 6CO₂ + 8H₂O. mol propanol = 9.20 g ÷ 60.09 g/mol = 0.1531 mol; mol O₂ = 0.1531 × 9/2 = 0.689 mol. 0.765 mol ignores the O already in propanol (ratio 5 : 1); 1.38 mol uses 9 without dividing by 2; 0.153 and 0.306 mol use unbalanced 1 : 1 and 2 : 1 ratios.",
 ["Balance C, then H, then O last - remember propanol already contains one O.","The O₂ : C₃H₈O ratio is 9 : 2.","0.1531 mol × 4.5."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q1"}],

/* ---------- Gym 4: solutions ---------- */
["m4-p-001",4,5,3,"Trap","mcq",
 "Exactly 106.5 g of Al(NO₃)₃ is dissolved in enough water to make 1.000 L. What is the combined concentration of all the ions in solution?",
 ["0.5000 M","1.000 M","1.500 M","2.000 M","6.500 M"],3,
 "Molar mass of Al(NO₃)₃ = 26.98 + 3(14.01 + 3 × 16.00) = 213.01 g/mol, so 106.5 g/L = 0.5000 M. Each formula unit dissolves into 1 Al³⁺ + 3 NO₃⁻ = 4 ions: 4 × 0.5000 M = 2.000 M of ions. 0.5000 M is the salt itself; 1.500 M counts only the nitrate; 1.000 M treats the salt as two ions; 6.500 M counts all 13 atoms, splitting the nitrate ions apart (they stay intact in water).",
 ["First find the molarity of the salt.","How many ions does one Al(NO₃)₃ release? Nitrate stays together.","0.5000 M × 4."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q34"}],
["m4-p-002",4,5,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "50.0 mL of 0.200 M CaCl₂ is mixed with 50.0 mL of 0.100 M NaCl, and no reaction occurs. Assuming the volumes add, find the combined concentration of every ion present after mixing.",
 ["0.150 M","0.400 M","0.250 M","0.800 M","0.300 M"],1,
 "Moles of ions: CaCl₂: 0.0500 L × 0.200 M = 0.0100 mol → 0.0100 mol Ca²⁺ + 0.0200 mol Cl⁻. NaCl: 0.0500 L × 0.100 M = 0.00500 mol → 0.00500 mol Na⁺ + 0.00500 mol Cl⁻. Total = 0.0400 mol of ions in 0.1000 L = 0.400 M. 0.800 M adds the undiluted ion concentrations (forgets that the volume doubled); 0.150 M counts formula units instead of ions; 0.250 M is Cl⁻ alone; 0.300 M treats CaCl₂ as only two ions.",
 ["Work in moles of ions, then divide by the TOTAL volume.","CaCl₂ releases 3 ions, NaCl releases 2.","0.0400 mol ÷ 0.1000 L."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q34"}],

/* ---------- Gym 5: thermochemistry (Evening Exam 2) ---------- */
["m5-p-001",5,7,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "A gas sealed in a piston absorbs 245 cal of heat from a hot plate, and while expanding it does 410 cal of work pushing the piston outward. What is ΔE for the gas?",
 ["+655 cal","−165 cal","+165 cal","−655 cal","−165 J"],1,
 "ΔE = q + w, with signs from the system's point of view. Heat absorbed: q = +245 cal. Work done BY the gas: w = −410 cal. ΔE = 245 + (−410) = −165 cal. +655 cal treats the work as done on the gas; +165 cal flips both signs; −655 cal makes the absorbed heat negative too; −165 J has the right number with the wrong unit (the data are in calories).",
 ["Decide each sign from the gas's point of view.","Heat absorbed is positive; work done by the gas is negative.","245 − 410."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q10"}],
["m5-p-002",5,7,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "A gas absorbs 825 J of heat while it expands from 2.00 L to 6.50 L against a constant external pressure of 1.50 atm. What is ΔE of the gas? (1 L·atm = 101.3 J)",
 ["+1509 J","+818 J","−141 J","+141 J","−1509 J"],3,
 "w = −PΔV = −(1.50 atm)(6.50 L − 2.00 L) = −6.75 L·atm × 101.3 J/(L·atm) = −684 J (expanding, the gas does work). q = +825 J. ΔE = 825 + (−684) = +141 J. +1509 J uses w = +684 J (sign of expansion work reversed); +818 J subtracts 6.75 without converting L·atm to J; −141 J flips both signs; −1509 J makes both q and w negative.",
 ["Expansion work: w = −PΔV, and ΔV is positive here.","Convert L·atm to J before adding to q.","825 J − 684 J."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q10"}],
["m5-p-003",5,9,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which substance has ΔH°f = 0 at 25 °C and 1 atm?",
 ["O₃(g)","C(diamond)","Br₂(g)","H₂O(l)","Br₂(l)"],4,
 "ΔH°f is zero only for an element in its standard state - its most stable form at 25 °C and 1 atm. Bromine is a liquid at room temperature, so Br₂(l) is the reference form. Br₂(g) is the right element in the wrong phase; O₃ and diamond are not the most stable forms of oxygen (O₂) and carbon (graphite); H₂O(l) is a compound (−285.8 kJ/mol).",
 ["Zero is reserved for one particular form of each element.","Ask: is it an element, and is it in its most stable state at 25 °C?","Which halogen is a liquid at room temperature?"],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q11"}],
["m5-p-004",5,9,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which reaction has ΔH°rxn equal to the standard enthalpy of formation of methanol, CH₃OH(l)?",
 ["CO(g) + 2H₂(g) → CH₃OH(l)","C(graphite) + 4H(g) + O(g) → CH₃OH(l)","C(graphite) + 2H₂(g) + ½O₂(g) → CH₃OH(l)","2C(graphite) + 4H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2CH₃OH(l)","C(diamond) + 2H₂(g) + ½O₂(g) → CH₃OH(l)"],2,
 "A formation reaction makes exactly ONE mole of the compound from its elements, each in its standard state: C(graphite), H₂(g), O₂(g). A fractional coefficient (½O₂) is allowed so that the product stays at 1 mol. The CO reaction starts from a compound; H(g) and O(g) atoms are not standard states; the doubled equation makes 2 mol (its ΔH is 2 × ΔH°f); diamond is not carbon's standard state.",
 ["Formation: elements → 1 mol of compound.","Each element must be in its most stable form at 25 °C and 1 atm.","Fractions are fine if they keep the product at one mole."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q11"}],
["m5-p-005",5,7,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Hot copper (95.0 °C) is lowered into 150.0 g of water at 22.0 °C inside an insulated cup, and everything settles at 26.5 °C. How many grams of copper were added? (c(Cu) = 0.385 J/(g·K), c(water) = 4.184 J/(g·°C))",
 ["9.85 g","77.2 g","100. g","107 g","1.63 × 10³ g"],3,
 "Heat gained by the water = heat lost by the copper. q(water) = (150.0 g)(4.184 J/(g·°C))(26.5 − 22.0 °C) = 2824 J. For the copper ΔT = 26.5 − 95.0 = −68.5 °C, so −2824 J = m(0.385)(−68.5) → m = 2824 ÷ (0.385 × 68.5) = 107 g. 100. g uses 95.0 − 22.0 (the water's starting temperature, not the shared final one); 77.2 g uses 95.0 °C as the ΔT; 9.85 g puts water's specific heat on the copper; 1.63 × 10³ g assumes both objects changed by the same number of degrees.",
 ["Heat lost by the metal = heat gained by the water; both end at 26.5 °C.","Find q(water) from m·c·ΔT, then solve m·c·ΔT for the copper.","2824 J ÷ (0.385 × 68.5)."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q12"}],
["m5-p-006",5,7,4,"Exam","num",
 "A 45.0 g iron bolt at 150.0 °C is dropped into 120.0 g of water at 20.0 °C in an insulated cup. What is the final temperature of the bolt and water? (c(Fe) = 0.449 J/(g·°C), c(water) = 4.184 J/(g·°C)) Enter a number in °C.",
 ["25.0 °C"],25.03,
 "Heat lost by the iron = heat gained by the water: (45.0)(0.449)(150.0 − T) = (120.0)(4.184)(T − 20.0). That is 20.2(150.0 − T) = 502.1(T − 20.0), so 3031 + 10,042 = (502.1 + 20.2)T and T = 13,073 ÷ 522.3 = 25.0 °C. The final temperature lands close to the water's start because the water's heat capacity (502 J/°C) dwarfs the bolt's (20.2 J/°C). Simply averaging 150 and 20 (85 °C) ignores the heat capacities.",
 ["Both objects end at the same temperature T.","Write m·c·(150.0 − T) for the iron = m·c·(T − 20.0) for the water.","Solve 20.2(150.0 − T) = 502.1(T − 20.0)."],{abs:0.2,src:"2018 FALL Final Q12"}],
["m5-p-007",5,9,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Hydrogen peroxide decomposes: 2H₂O₂(l) → 2H₂O(l) + O₂(g). Using ΔH°f[H₂O₂(l)] = −187.8 kJ/mol and ΔH°f[H₂O(l)] = −285.8 kJ/mol, what is ΔH when 20.0 g of H₂O₂ decomposes at constant pressure?",
 ["−196 kJ","−115 kJ","−109 kJ","−57.6 kJ","+57.6 kJ"],3,
 "ΔH°rxn = 2(−285.8) + 0 − 2(−187.8) = −196.0 kJ for 2 mol of H₂O₂ (O₂ is an element, so 0). mol H₂O₂ = 20.0 g ÷ 34.02 g/mol = 0.588 mol. ΔH = 0.588 mol × (−196.0 kJ / 2 mol) = −57.6 kJ. −196 kJ is the value for the equation as written and ignores the 20.0 g; −115 kJ forgets that −196 kJ belongs to 2 mol; −109 kJ uses water's molar mass; +57.6 kJ subtracts products from reactants.",
 ["ΔH°rxn = Σ n·ΔH°f(products) − Σ m·ΔH°f(reactants).","That ΔH goes with 2 mol of H₂O₂; scale it to the moles in 20.0 g.","0.588 mol × (−98.0 kJ/mol)."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q11"}],
["m5-p-008",5,9,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Ammonia is oxidized: 4NH₃(g) + 5O₂(g) → 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g). ΔH°f values (kJ/mol): NH₃(g) −45.9, NO(g) 90.3, H₂O(g) −241.8. What is ΔH when 12.0 g of O₂ is consumed?",
 ["−84.9 kJ","−68.0 kJ","−340 kJ","−39.6 kJ","+68.0 kJ"],1,
 "ΔH°rxn = [4(90.3) + 6(−241.8)] − [4(−45.9) + 5(0)] = 361.2 − 1450.8 + 183.6 = −906.0 kJ per 5 mol of O₂. mol O₂ = 12.0 g ÷ 32.00 g/mol = 0.375 mol. ΔH = 0.375 × (−906.0 ÷ 5) = −68.0 kJ. −84.9 kJ divides by 4 (NH₃'s coefficient, not O₂'s); −340 kJ uses the whole −906 kJ per mole of O₂; −39.6 kJ leaves the coefficients out of the ΔH°f sum; +68.0 kJ has the sign reversed.",
 ["Get ΔH°rxn first, multiplying each ΔH°f by its coefficient.","The reaction's ΔH goes with 5 mol of O₂.","0.375 mol O₂ × (−906.0 kJ / 5 mol O₂)."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q11"}],
["m5-p-009",5,9,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Butane, C₄H₁₀(g), burns in excess oxygen to give CO₂(g) and H₂O(g). Write the balanced equation using the lowest whole-number coefficients, then find ΔH°rxn for that equation. ΔH°f (kJ/mol): C₄H₁₀(g) −125.6, CO₂(g) −393.5, H₂O(g) −241.8.",
 ["−2657 kJ","−5755 kJ","−5315 kJ","+5315 kJ","−5566 kJ"],2,
 "Balanced: 2C₄H₁₀ + 13O₂ → 8CO₂ + 10H₂O (with one C₄H₁₀ the O₂ coefficient would be 13/2). ΔH°rxn = [8(−393.5) + 10(−241.8)] − [2(−125.6) + 13(0)] = −3148.0 − 2418.0 + 251.2 = −5314.8 ≈ −5315 kJ. −2657 kJ is per mole of butane (fractional coefficients, not smallest whole numbers); −5755 kJ uses H₂O(l), −285.8, although the equation says gas; −5566 kJ forgets to subtract butane's ΔH°f; +5315 kJ is reactants minus products.",
 ["Balance C, then H, then O; double everything if O₂ comes out fractional.","Multiply each ΔH°f by its coefficient; O₂(g) is zero.","8(−393.5) + 10(−241.8) − 2(−125.6)."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q13"}],

/* ---------- Gym 6: light and quantum theory (Evening Exam 2) ---------- */
["m6-p-001",6,10,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Suppose each sodium atom in a 2.5 mg sample gives off exactly one 589 nm photon, as in the yellow glow of a street light. What is the combined energy of all those photons?",
 ["3.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","22 J","2.2 × 10⁴ J","2.2 × 10⁻⁸ J","3.7 × 10⁻²³ J"],1,
 "Atoms: 2.5 × 10⁻³ g ÷ 22.99 g/mol = 1.09 × 10⁻⁴ mol × 6.022 × 10²³ = 6.55 × 10¹⁹ atoms. One photon: E = hc/λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴)(2.998 × 10⁸) ÷ (589 × 10⁻⁹ m) = 3.37 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Total = 6.55 × 10¹⁹ × 3.37 × 10⁻¹⁹ J = 22 J (2 sig figs, from 2.5 mg). 3.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J is a single photon; 2.2 × 10⁴ J treats mg as g; 2.2 × 10⁻⁸ J leaves λ in nm; 3.7 × 10⁻²³ J multiplies moles (not atoms) by the photon energy.",
 ["Total energy = (number of photons) × (energy of one photon).","Number of photons = number of Na atoms: mg → g → mol → atoms.","6.55 × 10¹⁹ × 3.37 × 10⁻¹⁹ J."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q6"}],
["m6-p-002",6,11,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "A sample of 0.0100 mol of hydrogen atoms is excited, and every atom then emits exactly one photon as its electron falls from n = 3 to n = 2. Using Eₙ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J (1/n²), what total energy is released?",
 ["3.03 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","3.03 × 10⁻²¹ J","1.46 × 10³ J","1.82 × 10³ J","2.19 × 10³ J"],3,
 "One photon: |ΔE| = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J × (1/2² − 1/3²) = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ × 0.1389 = 3.03 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Atoms: 0.0100 mol × 6.022 × 10²³ = 6.022 × 10²¹. Total = 6.022 × 10²¹ × 3.03 × 10⁻¹⁹ J = 1.82 × 10³ J released. 3.03 × 10⁻¹⁹ J is one photon; 3.03 × 10⁻²¹ J multiplies by moles instead of atoms; 2.19 × 10³ J uses 1/2 − 1/3 without squaring; 1.46 × 10³ J uses only the n = 3 level energy.",
 ["Find the energy of ONE photon from the Bohr formula first.","Square the n values: 1/4 − 1/9.","Then multiply by the number of atoms, 0.0100 × 6.022 × 10²³."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q6"}],
["m6-p-003",6,11,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Hydrogen atoms are excited so that electrons occupy levels up to n = 4. Counting every possible downward jump among the levels n = 1, 2, 3 and 4, how many distinct lines can appear in the emission spectrum?",
 ["6","3","4","10","12"],0,
 "List the jumps: 4→3, 4→2, 4→1, 3→2, 3→1, 2→1 = 6. In general, N levels give N(N − 1)/2 lines: 4 × 3 ÷ 2 = 6. 3 counts only the jumps that start from n = 4; 4 is the number of levels; 12 counts each pair twice; 10 is the count for five levels.",
 ["Each line comes from one pair of levels (upper → lower).","List every pair, starting from n = 4 and working down.","N(N − 1)/2 with N = 4."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q7"}],
["m6-p-004",6,11,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "A sample of hydrogen atoms is excited to n = 5. Of all the distinct lines the sample can emit as electrons fall back down, how many belong to the visible Balmer series (transitions that END at n = 2)?",
 ["1","3","4","6","10"],1,
 "Balmer lines end at n = 2 and can start from any higher level the electrons occupy: 5→2, 4→2 and 3→2 = 3 lines. 10 is the total number of lines from five levels (5 × 4 ÷ 2); 4 also counts 2→1, which ends at n = 1 (Lyman, ultraviolet); 6 is the total for four levels; 1 counts only the jump straight from n = 5.",
 ["Which final level defines the Balmer series?","Starting levels above n = 2 that electrons can occupy here: 3, 4, 5.","One line per starting level."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q7"}],
["m6-p-005",6,10,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "A violet laser emits light at 405 nm. How much energy does a mole of these photons carry?",
 ["4.90 × 10⁻¹⁹ kJ/mol","295 kJ/mol","2.95 × 10⁵ kJ/mol","8.14 × 10⁻⁴⁶ kJ/mol","2.95 × 10⁻⁷ kJ/mol"],1,
 "One photon: E = hc/λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s)(2.998 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (405 × 10⁻⁹ m) = 4.90 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Per mole: × 6.022 × 10²³ = 2.95 × 10⁵ J/mol = 295 kJ/mol. 4.90 × 10⁻¹⁹ is the per-photon energy in J, not per mole; 2.95 × 10⁵ forgets J → kJ; 8.14 × 10⁻⁴⁶ divides by Avogadro's number; 2.95 × 10⁻⁷ leaves λ in nm.",
 ["Find the energy of one photon, E = hc/λ, with λ in metres.","Multiply by 6.022 × 10²³ for a mole.","Divide by 1000 to get kJ."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q4"}],
["m6-p-006",6,10,3,"Exam","num",
 "Breaking a certain chemical bond takes 182 kJ/mol. What is the longest wavelength of light whose photons each carry enough energy to break one such bond? Enter a number in nm.",
 ["657 nm"],657.3,
 "Per bond (per photon): 182 × 10³ J/mol ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹ = 3.022 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. λ = hc/E = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴)(2.998 × 10⁸) ÷ (3.022 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 6.57 × 10⁻⁷ m = 657 nm. Longer wavelengths carry less energy per photon, so 657 nm is the limit. The trap is skipping the per-mole → per-photon step: λ = hc/E only works with the energy of ONE photon.",
 ["λ = hc/E needs the energy of ONE photon.","kJ/mol → J/mol (× 1000) → J per bond (÷ 6.022 × 10²³).","Then λ in m × 10⁹ = nm."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q4"}],
["m6-p-007",6,11,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Light of wavelength 97.20 nm is absorbed by a hydrogen atom whose electron starts in n = 1. Find the n value of the level the electron ends up in. (Eₙ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J/n²)",
 ["n = 2","n = 3","n = 8","n = 5","n = 4"],4,
 "Photon energy: E = hc/λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴)(2.998 × 10⁸) ÷ (97.20 × 10⁻⁹ m) = 2.044 × 10⁻¹⁸ J. Absorbed from n = 1: 2.044 × 10⁻¹⁸ = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ (1 − 1/n²) → 1/n² = 1 − 0.9376 = 0.0624 → n² = 16 → n = 4. Check: 1 → 3 needs 102.5 nm and 1 → 5 needs 94.9 nm; 1 → 8 would need 92.6 nm. A shorter wavelength means a bigger jump.",
 ["Convert the wavelength to the energy of one photon.","Set that energy equal to 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J (1/1² − 1/n²).","Solve for 1/n², then for n."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q5"}],
["m6-p-008",6,11,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "A hydrogen atom whose electron is in the n = 2 level takes in a 433.9 nm photon. Which level does the electron move up to? (Eₙ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J/n²)",
 ["n = 5","n = 4","n = 3","n = 6","n = 7"],0,
 "E(photon) = hc/λ = (1.986 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m) ÷ (433.9 × 10⁻⁹ m) = 4.578 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Starting from n = 2: 4.578 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ (1/2² − 1/n²) → 1/n² = 0.2500 − 0.2100 = 0.0400 → n = 5. Starting from n = 1 by mistake gives 1/n² = 0.79, which matches no level at all. For comparison, 2 → 4 needs 486.0 nm and 2 → 6 needs 410.1 nm.",
 ["The starting level is n = 2, not n = 1.","ΔE = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ (1/4 − 1/n²) for absorption from n = 2.","1/n² = 0.25 − ΔE ÷ (2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸)."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q5"}],
["m6-p-009",6,12,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "What is the total number of orbitals in the n = 5 shell of an atom?",
 ["5","9","16","25","50"],3,
 "Level n has n sublevels (l = 0 to n − 1) and n² orbitals: s 1 + p 3 + d 5 + f 7 + g 9 = 25. 5 counts sublevels; 9 counts only the l = 4 orbitals; 16 is the answer for n = 4; 50 is the maximum number of electrons (2n²).",
 ["List the l values allowed for n = 5.","Each l value gives 2l + 1 orbitals.","1 + 3 + 5 + 7 + 9."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q6"}],
["m6-p-010",6,12,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "What is the maximum number of electrons in one atom that can have both n = 4 and mₛ = −½?",
 ["4","8","7","32","16"],4,
 "n = 4 has n² = 16 orbitals (4s, 4p, 4d, 4f: 1 + 3 + 5 + 7). Each orbital holds two electrons of opposite spin, so exactly one electron per orbital can have mₛ = −½: 16 electrons. 32 is all the n = 4 electrons (both spins); 4 counts sublevels; 8 puts two per sublevel; 7 counts only the 4f orbitals.",
 ["How many orbitals have n = 4?","Each orbital holds one electron of each spin.","One −½ electron per orbital."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q6"}],
["m6-p-011",6,12,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "An electron is described by (n, l, mₗ, mₛ). Which of these combinations is impossible?",
 ["3, 2, −2, +½","2, 1, −1, −½","3, 1, +2, −½","4, 0, 0, +½","5, 3, 0, −½"],2,
 "mₗ must lie between −l and +l. For l = 1, mₗ can only be −1, 0 or +1, so mₗ = +2 is impossible. The others obey l ≤ n − 1 and |mₗ| ≤ l: (3, 2, −2) is a 3d electron, (2, 1, −1) a 2p, (4, 0, 0) a 4s and (5, 3, 0) a 5f, and mₛ is ±½ in each.",
 ["Check three rules: l < n, |mₗ| ≤ l, mₛ = ±½.","Test each set's mₗ against its l.","A p sublevel (l = 1) has only three orbitals."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q7"}],
["m6-p-012",6,10,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "The visible region runs from about 400 nm (violet) to 700 nm (red). Which wavelength lies in the infrared region of the spectrum?",
 ["98 nm","310 nm","3.0 × 10⁸ nm","560 nm","2.5 × 10³ nm"],4,
 "Infrared lies just beyond red: longer than about 700 nm but shorter than microwaves (roughly 1 mm = 10⁶ nm). 2.5 × 10³ nm (2.5 μm) fits. 98 nm and 310 nm are ultraviolet (shorter than violet); 560 nm is visible (green); 3.0 × 10⁸ nm = 0.30 m is in the microwave/radio range. In order of increasing wavelength: gamma < X-ray < UV < visible < IR < microwave < radio.",
 ["Infrared means 'below red' in energy - so LONGER than red in wavelength.","Order: UV < visible < IR < microwave.","Look for something somewhat longer than 700 nm but far shorter than a metre."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q28"}],
["m6-p-013",6,10,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Exciting the stretching vibration of the H–Br bond takes 5.0 × 10⁻²⁰ J per molecule. What is the wavelength of a photon with exactly this energy, and in which region of the spectrum does it lie?",
 ["4.0 × 10² nm, visible (violet)","4.0 × 10³ nm, infrared","4.0 × 10⁻⁶ nm, gamma ray","7.5 × 10¹³ nm, radio","4.0 × 10³ nm, ultraviolet"],1,
 "λ = hc/E = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴)(2.998 × 10⁸) ÷ (5.0 × 10⁻²⁰ J) = 4.0 × 10⁻⁶ m = 4.0 × 10³ nm, longer than red light (700 nm): infrared, the region that excites bond vibrations. 4.0 × 10² nm is a power-of-ten slip; 4.0 × 10⁻⁶ nm forgets that 4.0 × 10⁻⁶ is already in metres; 7.5 × 10¹³ is the frequency in Hz (E/h), not a wavelength; the last choice has the right number but puts a long wavelength in the UV.",
 ["λ = hc/E gives metres.","Convert m → nm (× 10⁹) and compare with 400-700 nm.","Longer than 700 nm but shorter than about a millimetre is infrared."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q28"}],

/* ---------- Gym 7: configurations and periodicity (Evening Exam 2) ---------- */
["m7-p-001",7,14,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Rank O, S, Al, Na and K from the smallest atomic radius to the largest.",
 ["O < S < Al < Na < K","O < S < Na < Al < K","S < O < Al < Na < K","O < Al < S < Na < K","K < Na < Al < S < O"],0,
 "Radius grows down a group (O < S; Na < K) and shrinks from left to right across a period (Na > Al > S). Combined: O (period 2) is smallest; then the period-3 atoms from right to left, S < Al < Na; then K (period 4, group 1) is largest. The second choice reverses Na and Al (as if radius grew across a period); S < O reverses the group trend; the last choice is the decreasing order.",
 ["Two trends: down a group and across a period.","Within period 3, which side has the bigger atoms?","Smallest at the upper right, largest at the lower left."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q8"}],
["m7-p-002",7,14,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Rank the period-2 atoms B, C, N, O and F by first ionization energy, from highest to lowest.",
 ["F > O > N > C > B","B > C > N > O > F","F > N > O > B > C","F > N > O > C > B","N > F > O > C > B"],3,
 "IE₁ rises across a period, with one dip that matters here: O is BELOW N, because O's fourth 2p electron is paired and the extra repulsion makes it easier to remove. Values (kJ/mol): F 1681, N 1402, O 1314, C 1086, B 801. F > O > N > C > B ignores the dip; B > C > … is the increasing order; F > N > O > B > C swaps B and C; N > F > … puts N above F.",
 ["General trend: IE₁ increases from left to right.","Look for the group 15 → group 16 exception.","N has a half-filled 2p set; O must pair one electron."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q9"}],
["m7-p-003",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which correctly ranks the first ionization energies of these period-3 elements from LOWEST to HIGHEST? Na, Mg, Al, P, S",
 ["Na < Mg < Al < P < S","Na < Al < Mg < P < S","Na < Mg < Al < S < P","S < P < Al < Mg < Na","Na < Al < Mg < S < P"],4,
 "The rising trend has two dips in period 3. Al (578 kJ/mol) is below Mg (738) because Al's electron comes from a higher-energy 3p orbital; S (1000) is below P (1012) because S loses a paired 3p electron. With Na lowest (496): Na < Al < Mg < S < P. Each other ordering misses one or both dips, or is reversed.",
 ["Start from the across-the-period trend, then fix the exceptions.","Group 13 dips below group 2; group 16 dips below group 15.","Swap Mg/Al and P/S in the naive order."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q9"}],
["m7-p-004",7,14,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Cobalt(III) ions, Co³⁺, occur in vitamin B₁₂. Which is the ion's ground-state configuration?",
 ["[Ar] 4s² 3d⁴","[Ar] 3d⁷","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵","[Ar] 3d⁶","[Ar] 4s² 3d⁷"],3,
 "Co (Z = 27) is [Ar] 4s² 3d⁷. Transition-metal cations lose their 4s electrons first, then 3d: remove both 4s electrons and one 3d → [Ar] 3d⁶ (24 electrons). [Ar] 4s² 3d⁴ removes 3d electrons first; [Ar] 3d⁷ removes only two electrons (that is Co²⁺); [Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵ borrows chromium's half-filled pattern; [Ar] 4s² 3d⁷ is the neutral atom.",
 ["Write neutral cobalt first.","Transition-metal cations lose 4s before 3d.","27 − 3 = 24 electrons: [Ar] plus 6 more."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q10"}],
["m7-p-005",7,14,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which species has the ground-state configuration [Ar] 3d⁵?",
 ["Fe³⁺","Mn (neutral atom)","Fe²⁺","Cr³⁺","Co²⁺"],0,
 "Fe (Z = 26) is [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶; losing the two 4s electrons and then one 3d electron gives Fe³⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁵. Fe²⁺ is [Ar] 3d⁶; neutral Mn is [Ar] 4s² 3d⁵ (its 4s electrons are still there); Cr³⁺ is [Ar] 3d³; Co²⁺ is [Ar] 3d⁷.",
 ["[Ar] 3d⁵ has 18 + 5 = 23 electrons.","Count each species' electrons: Z minus the charge.","Then check whether any 4s electrons remain."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q10"}],
["m7-p-006",7,14,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "The sequence K < Mg < Si < S < F is a correct least-to-greatest ranking for four of these properties. For which property is it WRONG?",
 ["First ionization energy","Electronegativity","Atomic radius","Nonmetallic character","Number of valence electrons"],2,
 "Going K → F moves up and to the right of the table. IE₁ (419, 738, 786, 1000, 1681 kJ/mol), electronegativity and nonmetallic character all increase toward the upper right, and the valence electrons go 1, 2, 4, 6, 7 - so those orders are correct. Atomic radius DEcreases toward the upper right, so for radius this order is backwards: F is the smallest and K the largest.",
 ["Locate each element: where is K, and where is F?","Most trends increase toward the upper right; one decreases.","Which property gets smaller as you go up and to the right?"],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q8"}],
["m7-p-007",7,14,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "A period-3 element has these successive ionization energies (kJ/mol): IE₁ 578, IE₂ 1817, IE₃ 2745, IE₄ 11,577, IE₅ 14,842. Which element is it?",
 ["Na","Mg","Si","Al","P"],3,
 "The huge jump comes between IE₃ (2745) and IE₄ (11,577): the fourth electron would have to come from the [Ne] core. So the element has 3 valence electrons - group 13, aluminum. Na would jump after IE₁, Mg after IE₂, Si after IE₄ and P after IE₅.",
 ["Find where the ratio between neighbouring IEs is largest.","The electrons removed before the jump are valence electrons.","Three valence electrons in period 3."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q9"}],
["m7-p-008",7,14,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Nickel forms the Ni²⁺ ion. Which choice gives the ion's ground-state configuration and correctly compares its radius with the Ni atom's?",
 ["[Ar] 4s² 3d⁶, smaller","[Ar] 3d⁸, larger","[Ar] 4s² 3d⁶, larger","[Ar] 4s² 3d⁸, the same size","[Ar] 3d⁸, smaller"],4,
 "Ni (Z = 28) is [Ar] 4s² 3d⁸; Ni²⁺ loses the two 4s electrons → [Ar] 3d⁸. Removing the outermost (n = 4) electrons empties that shell and leaves the same nuclear charge pulling on fewer electrons, so the cation is smaller. [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶ removes 3d electrons first (the classic trap), and a cation is never larger than its parent atom.",
 ["Write neutral Ni first, then remove the 4s electrons.","What happens to the size when the outermost shell is emptied?","Cations are smaller than their parent atoms."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q14"}],
["m7-p-009",7,14,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Arrange S²⁻, Cl⁻, K⁺ and Ca²⁺ in order of increasing radius (smallest first).",
 ["S²⁻ < Cl⁻ < K⁺ < Ca²⁺","Ca²⁺ < K⁺ < Cl⁻ < S²⁻","K⁺ < Ca²⁺ < S²⁻ < Cl⁻","Cl⁻ < S²⁻ < Ca²⁺ < K⁺","Ca²⁺ < K⁺ < S²⁻ < Cl⁻"],1,
 "All four ions have 18 electrons (isoelectronic with Ar). With the same number of electrons, more protons pull the cloud in tighter: Ca²⁺ (20 protons) < K⁺ (19) < Cl⁻ (17) < S²⁻ (16). The first choice is the reverse order; the others mix up which charge goes with which nuclear charge.",
 ["Count the electrons in each ion - what do you notice?","Same number of electrons, different numbers of protons.","More protons → smaller ion."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q14"}],

/* ---------- Gym 8: bonding ---------- */
["m8-p-001",8,16,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "From the average bond energies listed, what is the approximate ΔH for adding chlorine to ethylene, H₂C=CH₂(g) + Cl₂(g) → ClCH₂–CH₂Cl(g)? Bond energies (kJ/mol): C=C 614, C–C 347, C–Cl 339, Cl–Cl 243, C–H 413.",
 ["−168 kJ","+168 kJ","+171 kJ","+179 kJ","−411 kJ"],0,
 "Only the bonds that change matter (the four C–H bonds survive). Broken: C=C 614 + Cl–Cl 243 = 857 kJ. Formed: C–C 347 + 2 C–Cl (2 × 339 = 678) = 1025 kJ. ΔH ≈ 857 − 1025 = −168 kJ. +168 kJ is formed minus broken; +171 kJ forms only one C–Cl; +179 kJ forgets the new C–C single bond; −411 kJ forgets to break the Cl–Cl bond.",
 ["ΔH ≈ Σ(bonds broken) − Σ(bonds formed).","The C=C becomes a C–C, the Cl–Cl breaks, and two C–Cl bonds form.","857 − 1025."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q12"}],
["m8-p-002",8,16,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Rank Ge, P and Cl from lowest to highest electronegativity.",
 ["Cl < P < Ge","P < Ge < Cl","Ge < Cl < P","Ge < P < Cl","P < Cl < Ge"],3,
 "Electronegativity rises up and to the right. Ge (period 4, group 14) is lowest (1.8), P (period 3, group 15) is next (2.1), and Cl (period 3, group 17) is highest (3.0).",
 ["Electronegativity increases toward fluorine.","Ge is lower down and further left than P.","Cl is the furthest right."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q13"}],
["m8-p-003",8,16,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Propene, CH₂=CH–CH₃(g), burns to CO₂(g) and H₂O(g). With the average bond energies (kJ/mol) C–H 413, C–C 347, C=C 614, O=O 498, C=O (in CO₂) 799 and O–H 467, what is the approximate ΔH for burning 1 mol of propene?",
 ["−3832 kJ/mol","−4157 kJ/mol","−1916 kJ/mol","−2183 kJ/mol","+1916 kJ/mol"],2,
 "Per mole: C₃H₆ + 9/2 O₂ → 3CO₂ + 3H₂O. Broken: 1 C=C (614) + 1 C–C (347) + 6 C–H (2478) + 4.5 O=O (2241) = 5680 kJ. Formed: 6 C=O (4794) + 6 O–H (2802) = 7596 kJ. ΔH ≈ 5680 − 7596 = −1916 kJ/mol. −3832 kJ is for the doubled equation (2 mol of propene); −4157 kJ forgets to break the O=O bonds; −2183 kJ treats the C=C as a single bond; +1916 kJ is formed minus broken.",
 ["Draw propene: one C=C, one C–C and six C–H. Balance for 1 mol of propene.","Each CO₂ has two C=O; each H₂O has two O–H.","5680 − 7596."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q15"}],
["m8-p-004",8,16,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Approximate ΔH for burning 1 mol of ethanol vapour, CH₃CH₂OH(g) + 3O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g) + 3H₂O(g), from these average bond energies (kJ/mol): C–H 413, C–C 347, C–O 358, O–H 467, O=O 498, C=O (in CO₂) 799.",
 ["−1734 kJ","−1625 kJ","+1267 kJ","−1267 kJ","+331 kJ"],3,
 "Ethanol has 5 C–H, 1 C–C, 1 C–O and 1 O–H. Broken: 5(413) + 347 + 358 + 467 + 3(498) = 2065 + 347 + 358 + 467 + 1494 = 4731 kJ. Formed: 4 C=O (3196) + 6 O–H (2802) = 5998 kJ. ΔH ≈ 4731 − 5998 = −1267 kJ. −1734 kJ forgets ethanol's own O–H bond; −1625 kJ forgets the C–O bond; +1267 kJ is formed minus broken; +331 kJ counts only one C=O per CO₂.",
 ["Draw ethanol, CH₃–CH₂–O–H, and count every bond, including the O–H.","Products: 2 CO₂ (4 C=O) and 3 H₂O (6 O–H).","4731 − 5998."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q15"}],

/* ---------- Gym 9: Lewis structures and shape ---------- */
["m9-p-001",9,17,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "In the best Lewis structure of nitric acid, HNO₃ (H bonded to one O; N bonded to all three O atoms, with one N=O double bond), which formal charge belongs to N?",
 ["−1","0","+5","+2","+1"],4,
 "N has 5 valence electrons. In HNO₃ it has no lone pairs and four bonding pairs (one double bond + two single bonds = 8 bonding electrons). FC = 5 − (0 + ½ × 8) = +1. The single-bonded O that carries no H has −1, so the molecule is neutral overall. +5 is nitrogen's oxidation number, not its formal charge; 0 and −1 give N lone pairs it does not have; +2 counts only the three atoms attached, not the four bonding pairs.",
 ["FC = valence e⁻ − (lone-pair e⁻ + ½ bonding e⁻).","Count N's bonds: one double, two single; no lone pairs.","5 − 0 − 4."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q14"}],
["m9-p-002",9,17,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Nitrous oxide, N₂O, can be drawn as N=N=O (two double bonds; the terminal N and the O each carry two lone pairs). What are the formal charges on the terminal N, the central N and the O, in that order?",
 ["0, 0, 0","−1, +1, 0","0, +1, −1","+1, −1, 0","−1, 0, +1"],1,
 "Terminal N: 5 − (4 + ½·4) = −1. Central N: four bonding pairs and no lone pairs: 5 − (0 + ½·8) = +1. O: 6 − (4 + ½·4) = 0. Sum = 0, matching the neutral molecule. 0, +1, −1 belongs to the other resonance structure (N≡N–O), not this one; 0, 0, 0 is impossible because the central N has four bonds and no lone pairs.",
 ["Use FC = V − (L + ½B) for each atom separately.","The central N has four bonding pairs and no lone pairs.","Formal charges must add up to the overall charge (0)."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q14"}],
["m9-p-003",9,18,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Consider HCN, SO₂, I₃⁻ and OF₂. Which of them have a linear shape?",
 ["HCN only","HCN and I₃⁻","I₃⁻ only","SO₂ and OF₂","HCN, SO₂ and I₃⁻"],1,
 "HCN: C has two electron groups (a single and a triple bond) and no lone pairs → linear. I₃⁻: the central I has 2 bonds + 3 lone pairs = 5 groups; the lone pairs take the three equatorial positions, leaving the two outer I atoms 180° apart → linear. SO₂: 3 groups with one lone pair → bent. OF₂: 4 groups with two lone pairs → bent. A formula AX₂ is not automatically linear - count the lone pairs.",
 ["Draw each Lewis structure and count the electron groups on the central atom.","Lone pairs usually bend an AX₂ molecule - with one famous exception.","With 5 electron groups, lone pairs go equatorial."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q15"}],
["m9-p-004",9,18,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Count the species in this list whose atoms are all coplanar: BCl₃, H₂S, CH₄, XeF₄, PCl₃, SF₄, H₂CO",
 ["2","3","5","6","4"],4,
 "Planar: BCl₃ (trigonal planar), H₂S (bent - any three atoms define a plane), XeF₄ (square planar: 6 groups with the 2 lone pairs opposite each other) and H₂CO (trigonal planar around C) = 4. Not planar: CH₄ (tetrahedral), PCl₃ (trigonal pyramidal) and SF₄ (seesaw).",
 ["Find each species' molecular shape with VSEPR.","Any molecule with only three atoms is planar.","Planar shapes include trigonal planar, bent, square planar and T-shaped."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q18"}],
["m9-p-005",9,18,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "For how many of these species could one flat plane pass through every atom? ClF₃, CO₃²⁻, C₂H₆, NH₄⁺, SO₂, BrF₅",
 ["1","2","3","4","5"],2,
 "ClF₃: 5 groups with 2 lone pairs equatorial → T-shaped, and all four atoms lie in one plane. CO₃²⁻: trigonal planar. SO₂: bent, so planar. That makes 3. C₂H₆ has tetrahedral carbons; NH₄⁺ is tetrahedral; BrF₅ is square pyramidal (the apical F sits above the plane of the other four).",
 ["T-shaped and bent molecules are flat.","Count the electron groups on ClF₃ and BrF₅ carefully (expanded octets).","Tetrahedral centres are never planar."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q18"}],
["m9-p-006",9,17,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Consider a Lewis structure of the iodate ion, IO₃⁻, in which I forms three I–O single bonds and has one lone pair, and each O has three lone pairs. What is the formal charge on iodine in this structure?",
 ["+2","−1","0","+1","+5"],0,
 "I has 7 valence electrons. Here it has 2 lone-pair electrons and 3 bonds (6 bonding electrons): FC = 7 − (2 + ½·6) = +2. Each O: 6 − (6 + ½·2) = −1, so the total is +2 + 3(−1) = −1, the ion's charge. +5 is iodine's oxidation number, not its formal charge. Because these formal charges are large, structures with I=O double bonds (allowed for iodine, which can expand its octet) are favoured.",
 ["FC = V − (L + ½B).","Iodine: 7 valence electrons, one lone pair, three single bonds.","7 − 2 − 3."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q16"}],
["m9-p-007",9,17,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "For the chlorate ion, ClO₃⁻ (Cl central, expanded octet allowed, one lone pair on Cl in every structure), which Lewis structure has the most favourable formal charges?",
 ["Three Cl–O single bonds; formal charge on Cl = +2","One Cl=O double bond; formal charge on Cl = +1","Three Cl=O double bonds; formal charge on Cl = −1","Two Cl=O double bonds; formal charge on Cl = +1","Two Cl=O double bonds; formal charge on Cl = 0"],4,
 "26 valence electrons. Formal charge on Cl = 7 − 2 (lone pair) − (number of bonding pairs): three single bonds → +2; one double → +1; two double → 0; three double → −1. With two double bonds Cl is 0 and only the single-bonded O is −1. Three double bonds also leave a single −1, but it sits on Cl, the LESS electronegative atom - worse. 'Two double bonds, Cl = +1' miscounts by leaving out a bonding pair.",
 ["Cl formal charge = 7 − (lone-pair e⁻) − (number of bonding pairs).","Ideal: formal charges near zero, and any −1 on the most electronegative atom.","Compare the two-double-bond and three-double-bond structures."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q16"}],
["m9-p-008",9,17,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "In the nitrite ion, NO₂⁻, the two N–O bonds are identical because of resonance. Given typical lengths N–O = 136 pm and N=O = 122 pm, estimate the N–O bond length in NO₂⁻.",
 ["122 pm","125 pm","129 pm","131 pm","136 pm"],2,
 "The two resonance structures each have one N=O and one N–O, so each bond has order (2 + 1)/2 = 1.5 - halfway between single and double: (136 + 122)/2 = 129 pm. 131 pm uses bond order 4/3 (that belongs to nitrate or carbonate, with three O atoms); 122 and 136 pm pretend the bonds alternate; 125 pm is closer to a double bond than order 1.5 allows.",
 ["Draw both resonance structures of NO₂⁻.","Average bond order = total bonding pairs ÷ number of N–O links.","Order 1.5 sits halfway between the single and double bond lengths."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q17"}],

/* ---------- Gym 10: intermolecular forces and phases ---------- */
["m10-p-001",10,20,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "The boiling points of the halogens rise in the order F₂ < Cl₂ < Br₂ < I₂. What best explains this trend?",
 ["Larger electron clouds are more polarizable, so dispersion forces grow stronger","The molecules become more polar down the group","The X–X bond gets weaker down the group, so less energy is needed to boil","Heavier molecules move more slowly at the same temperature","Hydrogen bonding increases as the halogen gets larger"],0,
 "All four are nonpolar, so dispersion is the only intermolecular force. Down the group the molecules have more electrons in larger, more easily distorted (polarizable) clouds, giving stronger dispersion forces and higher boiling points. Boiling never breaks the X–X covalent bond; diatomic X₂ molecules have no dipole; they have no H to hydrogen bond; and boiling points reflect the attractions between molecules, not how fast heavy molecules move.",
 ["Boiling separates molecules - it does not break the bonds inside them.","These molecules are all nonpolar. Which force is left?","Bigger electron clouds distort more easily."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q16"}],
["m10-p-002",10,20,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Pentane, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃, boils at 36 °C, while its isomer neopentane, C(CH₃)₄, boils at 9.5 °C. Both have the formula C₅H₁₂. What best explains the difference?",
 ["Pentane has a larger molar mass","Pentane can hydrogen bond, neopentane cannot","Neopentane's C–C bonds are weaker, so it boils more easily","Neopentane is polar and pentane is nonpolar","Pentane's long chain has more surface contact with its neighbours, giving stronger dispersion forces"],4,
 "Same formula, same molar mass, same number of electrons - and both are nonpolar hydrocarbons with dispersion forces only. The difference is shape: rod-like pentane molecules lie alongside each other with lots of contact, while compact, nearly spherical neopentane touches its neighbours over a small area. More contact = stronger dispersion = higher boiling point. C–H hydrogens never hydrogen bond, and boiling breaks no covalent bonds.",
 ["Same formula means the same molar mass. What else can differ?","Picture a rod and a ball, each touching its neighbours.","Dispersion forces grow with contact area."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q16"}],
["m10-p-003",10,20,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Rank these three compounds from lowest to highest vapor pressure at room temperature: HOCH₂CH₂CH₂OH, CH₃CH₂CH₂OH, CH₃OCH₂CH₃",
 ["HOCH₂CH₂CH₂OH < CH₃CH₂CH₂OH < CH₃OCH₂CH₃","CH₃OCH₂CH₃ < CH₃CH₂CH₂OH < HOCH₂CH₂CH₂OH","HOCH₂CH₂CH₂OH < CH₃OCH₂CH₃ < CH₃CH₂CH₂OH","CH₃CH₂CH₂OH < HOCH₂CH₂CH₂OH < CH₃OCH₂CH₃","CH₃OCH₂CH₃ < HOCH₂CH₂CH₂OH < CH₃CH₂CH₂OH"],0,
 "Stronger intermolecular forces hold molecules in the liquid and LOWER the vapor pressure. The diol has two O–H groups (the most hydrogen bonding) → lowest vapor pressure. 1-Propanol has one O–H. The ether CH₃OCH₂CH₃ has no H on its O, so it cannot hydrogen bond with itself (dipole-dipole only) → highest vapor pressure. The second choice is the reverse - it ranks by increasing boiling point, not vapor pressure.",
 ["Stronger IMFs → lower vapor pressure.","Count the O–H groups that can hydrogen bond.","The ether's O has no H attached."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q17"}],
["m10-p-004",10,20,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Four liquids of nearly equal molar mass are compared at 25 °C. Rank them from lowest to highest vapor pressure: butane (C₄H₁₀, 58 g/mol), acetone ((CH₃)₂C=O, 58 g/mol), 1-propanol (CH₃CH₂CH₂OH, 60 g/mol), ethylene glycol (HOCH₂CH₂OH, 62 g/mol).",
 ["butane < acetone < 1-propanol < ethylene glycol","ethylene glycol < 1-propanol < acetone < butane","ethylene glycol < acetone < 1-propanol < butane","1-propanol < ethylene glycol < acetone < butane","ethylene glycol < 1-propanol < butane < acetone"],1,
 "The molar masses are nearly equal, so the type of force decides. Ethylene glycol: two O–H groups, extensive hydrogen bonding → lowest vapor pressure. 1-Propanol: one O–H, hydrogen bonding. Acetone: a polar C=O but no O–H → dipole-dipole only. Butane: nonpolar, dispersion only → highest vapor pressure. (Boiling points: 197, 97, 56 and −1 °C.)",
 ["With similar molar masses, compare the strongest force each molecule has.","Hydrogen bonding needs H on N, O or F.","Weakest forces → highest vapor pressure."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q17"}],
["m10-p-005",10,20,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "In which pure liquid are dispersion forces the ONLY attractions between molecules?",
 ["NF₃","SO₂","CH₃Cl","H₂S","BF₃"],4,
 "BF₃ is trigonal planar with three identical B–F bonds whose dipoles cancel → nonpolar → dispersion only. NF₃ is trigonal pyramidal (lone pair on N) → polar. SO₂ and H₂S are bent → polar. CH₃Cl is tetrahedral but has different atoms around C → polar. Polar bonds alone do not make a polar molecule; the shape decides.",
 ["Dispersion only means the molecule is nonpolar.","Use the VSEPR shape: do the bond dipoles cancel?","A lone pair on the central atom usually breaks the symmetry."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q18"}],
["m10-p-006",10,20,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Four straight-chain alkanes sit in open dishes at 25 °C: hexane, octane, pentane and heptane. Rank them from lowest to highest vapor pressure.",
 ["pentane < hexane < heptane < octane","octane < heptane < hexane < pentane","octane < hexane < heptane < pentane","hexane < pentane < octane < heptane","heptane < octane < pentane < hexane"],1,
 "All four are nonpolar, so dispersion forces are the only attractions, and they grow with chain length (more electrons, more surface contact). Octane has the strongest forces → lowest vapor pressure; pentane the weakest → highest. The first choice is the order of increasing boiling point, which is decreasing vapor pressure.",
 ["Longer chain → stronger dispersion forces.","Stronger forces → lower vapor pressure.","Start with the longest chain."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q19"}],
["m10-p-007",10,20,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Rank from lowest to highest vapor pressure at 25 °C: ethanol (46 g/mol), pentane (72 g/mol), 1-butanol (74 g/mol), propane (44 g/mol).",
 ["1-butanol < ethanol < pentane < propane","propane < ethanol < pentane < 1-butanol","1-butanol < pentane < ethanol < propane","ethanol < 1-butanol < propane < pentane","1-butanol < ethanol < propane < pentane"],0,
 "The alcohols hydrogen bond; the alkanes have only dispersion forces. 1-Butanol (hydrogen bonding plus a longer chain) has the lowest vapor pressure, then ethanol. Pentane is heavier than ethanol but cannot hydrogen bond, so it evaporates far more readily (bp 36 °C against 78 °C for ethanol). Small, nonpolar propane has the highest vapor pressure (it is a gas at room temperature). Ranking by molar mass alone puts pentane below ethanol - that is the trap.",
 ["Which of these molecules can hydrogen bond?","Within each type, compare by size.","Hydrogen bonding outweighs a modest difference in molar mass."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q19"}],
["m10-p-008",10,20,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Rank the hydrogen halides HBr, HF, HI and HCl from lowest to highest boiling point.",
 ["HF < HCl < HBr < HI","HCl < HBr < HI < HF","HI < HBr < HCl < HF","HCl < HBr < HF < HI","HF < HI < HBr < HCl"],1,
 "HCl, HBr and HI have dipole-dipole and dispersion forces; dispersion grows with size, so their boiling points rise HCl (−85 °C) < HBr (−67 °C) < HI (−35 °C). HF is the outlier: H bonded to F hydrogen bonds strongly, so HF boils highest (+20 °C) despite being the lightest. HF < HCl < HBr < HI follows molar mass alone and misses hydrogen bonding.",
 ["Which of the four can hydrogen bond?","For the others, bigger molecules have stronger dispersion forces.","The lightest one is the exception."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q20"}],
["m10-p-009",10,20,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Put CH₄, NH₃, H₂O and HF in order from lowest to highest boiling point.",
 ["CH₄ < NH₃ < H₂O < HF","CH₄ < NH₃ < HF < H₂O","HF < H₂O < NH₃ < CH₄","NH₃ < CH₄ < HF < H₂O","CH₄ < HF < NH₃ < H₂O"],1,
 "CH₄ is nonpolar (dispersion only) → lowest (−162 °C). NH₃, HF and H₂O all hydrogen bond. NH₃ (−33 °C) has the weakest hydrogen bonds (N is the least electronegative of the three). HF (+20 °C) forms strong hydrogen bonds, but each HF has only one H, so it averages about two hydrogen bonds per molecule. H₂O (100 °C) has two H atoms AND two lone pairs, so each molecule can form up to four hydrogen bonds - a 3-D network that gives the highest boiling point. Ranking purely by electronegativity puts HF on top - the trap.",
 ["Three of the four hydrogen bond; one cannot.","The strength of each hydrogen bond matters, but so does the NUMBER per molecule.","Water has two H donors and two lone pairs."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q20"}],
["m10-p-010",10,21,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "At 19.0 °C the vapor above liquid X exerts 40.0 torr. Given ΔH(vap) = 42.3 kJ/mol, at what temperature does X boil under 1 atm?",
 ["−23 °C","19 °C","42 °C","78 °C","352 °C"],3,
 "At the normal boiling point P₂ = 760 torr. ln(P₂/P₁) = −(ΔH(vap)/R)(1/T₂ − 1/T₁) with T₁ = 292.15 K. ln(760/40.0) = 2.944, so 1/T₂ = 1/292.15 − (8.314 × 2.944)/42,300 = 3.4229 × 10⁻³ − 5.787 × 10⁻⁴ = 2.844 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹ → T₂ = 351.6 K = 78 °C. −23 °C comes from a sign error (the boiling point must be above 19 °C, where P is still below 1 atm); 42 °C uses log₁₀ instead of ln; 19 °C uses R = 0.08206 with ΔH in J; 352 °C forgets to convert kelvin back to °C.",
 ["The normal boiling point is where the vapor pressure reaches 760 torr.","Clausius-Clapeyron: T in kelvin, ΔH in J/mol, R = 8.314 J/(mol·K).","Solve for 1/T₂, invert, then subtract 273.15."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q32"}],
["m10-p-011",10,21,4,"Exam","num",
 "Benzene has a normal boiling point of 80.1 °C and ΔH(vap) = 30.8 kJ/mol. Use the Clausius-Clapeyron equation to estimate the vapor pressure of benzene at 25.0 °C. Enter a number in torr.",
 ["109 torr"],109.4,
 "Known point: P₁ = 760 torr at T₁ = 353.25 K; T₂ = 298.15 K. ln(P₂/760) = −(30,800/8.314)(1/298.15 − 1/353.25) = −3704.6 × 5.232 × 10⁻⁴ = −1.938. P₂ = 760 × exp(−1.938) = 109 torr. Sanity check: 25 °C is below the boiling point, so the vapor pressure must be below 760 torr. Common slips: Celsius temperatures, ΔH in kJ with R in J, or log₁₀ in place of ln.",
 ["The normal boiling point gives a known pair: 760 torr at 80.1 °C.","ln(P₂/P₁) = −(ΔH/R)(1/T₂ − 1/T₁), temperatures in K.","ln(P₂/760) ≈ −1.94."],{tol:0.02,src:"2017 FALL Final Q32"}],
["m10-p-012",10,21,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "A substance's phase diagram has its triple point at −40 °C and 0.50 atm and its critical point at 150 °C and 45 atm. At 1.00 atm it melts at −38 °C and boils at 60 °C. Which phase is most stable at 25 °C and 1.00 atm?",
 ["Solid","Gas","Liquid","Supercritical fluid","Solid and liquid in equilibrium"],2,
 "At 1.00 atm the substance is solid below its normal melting point (−38 °C), liquid between −38 °C and its normal boiling point (60 °C), and gas above 60 °C. 25 °C falls in the liquid range. A supercritical fluid needs both T above 150 °C and P above 45 atm; two phases coexist only exactly on a boundary line (−38 °C or 60 °C at this pressure).",
 ["At a fixed pressure, read along a horizontal line of the phase diagram.","Where does 25 °C fall relative to the normal melting and boiling points?","Between melting and boiling is the liquid."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q36"}],
["m10-p-013",10,21,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "For substance Z, points on the liquid-gas boundary of the phase diagram are 10 °C, 8.5 atm; 25 °C, 12 atm; and 40 °C, 17 atm. Its triple point is −60 °C, 2.0 atm and its critical point is 95 °C, 42 atm. What is the minimum pressure needed to liquefy gaseous Z at 25 °C?",
 ["1.0 atm","2.0 atm","8.5 atm","42 atm","12 atm"],4,
 "At 25 °C, raise the pressure along a vertical line until it meets the liquid-gas boundary: at 25 °C that boundary is at 12 atm (the vapor pressure of liquid Z at 25 °C). At any higher pressure Z is a liquid. 2.0 atm is the triple-point pressure (at a different temperature); 42 atm is the critical pressure, which only matters near 95 °C; 8.5 atm is the boundary at 10 °C, not 25 °C.",
 ["Liquefying at constant temperature means moving straight up on the diagram.","Where does the vertical line at 25 °C meet the liquid-gas curve?","Read the boundary point given for 25 °C."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q35"}],
["m10-p-014",10,20,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "In paper chromatography the paper (cellulose, full of O–H groups) is very polar, and the solvent used here is fairly nonpolar. A mixture of compounds A and B is spotted on the paper; after the run the solvent front is 8.0 cm from the start, spot A has moved 2.0 cm and spot B 6.5 cm. Which statement is true?",
 ["Rf(A) > Rf(B), because A is more polar","Rf(A) < Rf(B), because A is more polar","Rf(A) > Rf(B), because A is less polar","Rf(A) < Rf(B), because A is less polar","Rf(A) = Rf(B), because both moved in the same solvent"],1,
 "Rf = distance moved by the spot ÷ distance moved by the solvent: A = 2.0/8.0 = 0.25 and B = 6.5/8.0 = 0.81. A lags because it is attracted more strongly to the polar paper (dipole-dipole forces or hydrogen bonding), so A is the more polar compound. The less polar B prefers the moving, nonpolar solvent and travels further.",
 ["Rf = spot distance ÷ solvent-front distance.","Which compound 'sticks' to the polar paper?","Polar compounds cling to polar paper."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q37"}],
["m10-p-015",10,20,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "A paper-chromatography run uses polar cellulose paper and a nonpolar solvent (hexane). Which of these compounds would have the LARGEST Rf value?",
 ["Glycerol, HOCH₂CH(OH)CH₂OH","Ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH","1-Butanol, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH","Acetic acid, CH₃COOH","Octane, C₈H₁₈"],4,
 "The largest Rf belongs to the compound least attracted to the polar paper and most at home in the nonpolar solvent - the least polar compound. Octane is a nonpolar hydrocarbon with no O–H group, so it travels with the hexane almost to the front. Glycerol, with three O–H groups, barely moves; the other alcohols and acetic acid hydrogen bond to the cellulose and lag behind.",
 ["A large Rf means the compound travels far with the solvent.","A nonpolar solvent carries nonpolar compounds best.","Which compound cannot hydrogen bond at all?"],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q37"}],

/* ---------- Gym 11: solutions, colligative properties, biomolecules ---------- */
["m11-p-001",11,23,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Lactose, C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁, forms when a glucose molecule and a galactose molecule (each C₆H₁₂O₆) join. Which statement about this reaction is correct?",
 ["It is a hydrolysis: one H₂O is consumed, so lactose would be C₁₂H₂₆O₁₃","No small molecule is released, so lactose should be C₁₂H₂₄O₁₂","It releases O₂, so lactose has fewer O atoms than the two sugars","It is a condensation: one H₂O is released, so lactose (342.3 g/mol) is 18.0 g/mol lighter than the two sugars combined","Two H₂O molecules are released, one from each sugar"],3,
 "C₆H₁₂O₆ + C₆H₁₂O₆ = C₁₂H₂₄O₁₂ (360.3 g/mol). Lactose is C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ - exactly one H₂O fewer. Joining two sugars through a C–O–C (glycosidic) link is a condensation (dehydration) reaction that releases one water: 2(180.16) − 18.02 = 342.30 g/mol. Hydrolysis is the reverse (water added to split the link). Releasing two waters would give C₁₂H₂₀O₁₀.",
 ["Add the two sugar formulas and compare with lactose.","What is the difference between C₁₂H₂₄O₁₂ and C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁?","Losing H₂O to join two molecules is called condensation."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q28"}],
["m11-p-002",11,23,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Three glucose molecules (C₆H₁₂O₆) join in a chain by condensation reactions to form a trisaccharide. What is its molecular formula?",
 ["C₁₈H₃₆O₁₈","C₁₈H₃₄O₁₇","C₁₈H₃₀O₁₅","C₁₈H₃₂O₁₆","C₁₈H₃₂O₁₈"],3,
 "Three glucose units = C₁₈H₃₆O₁₈. Linking three units in a chain takes two links, and each link releases one H₂O: C₁₈H₃₆O₁₈ − 2H₂O = C₁₈H₃₂O₁₆. C₁₈H₃₄O₁₇ removes only one water; C₁₈H₃₀O₁₅ removes three (one per sugar - but n units need only n − 1 links); C₁₈H₃₂O₁₈ removes H₂ instead of H₂O.",
 ["How many links join three units in a chain?","Each link releases one water molecule.","C₁₈H₃₆O₁₈ minus 2 H₂O."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q28"}],
["m11-p-003",11,23,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which interaction keeps the paired strands of DNA from separating?",
 ["Covalent bonds between the deoxyribose sugars of opposite strands","Ionic attractions between the phosphate groups of the two strands","Hydrogen bonds between bases paired A with G and C with T","Dispersion forces between the phosphate backbones only","Hydrogen bonds between bases paired A with T and G with C"],4,
 "The sugar-phosphate backbones are on the outside; the bases point inward and pair by hydrogen bonding: adenine with thymine (2 hydrogen bonds) and guanine with cytosine (3), with stacking (dispersion) forces between neighbouring base pairs. The phosphates are negatively charged and repel each other rather than attract. A-G and C-T is the wrong pairing.",
 ["The backbones are on the outside; what points inward?","The interaction is an intermolecular force, not a covalent bond.","Which bases are complementary?"],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q29"}],
["m11-p-004",11,23,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Proteins are polymers. What are their monomer units?",
 ["Nucleotides","Monosaccharides","Fatty acids","Amino acids","Ethene molecules"],3,
 "Proteins are chains of amino acids joined by peptide (amide) bonds. Nucleotides build DNA and RNA; monosaccharides build polysaccharides such as starch and cellulose; fatty acids are parts of lipids (which are not polymers); ethene is the monomer of the plastic polyethylene.",
 ["Think of a protein's primary structure - a sequence of what?","Peptide bonds join these units.","About twenty kinds of them occur in living things."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q32"}],
["m11-p-005",11,23,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Glycine (H₂N–CH₂–COOH, C₂H₅NO₂) and alanine (H₂N–CH(CH₃)–COOH, C₃H₇NO₂) combine to make the dipeptide Gly-Ala. Which describes the dipeptide?",
 ["One peptide bond, –C(=O)–NH–, and formula C₅H₁₀N₂O₃","One peptide bond and formula C₅H₁₂N₂O₄","An ester link, –C(=O)–O–, and formula C₅H₁₀N₂O₃","Two peptide bonds and formula C₅H₈N₂O₂","One peptide bond and formula C₅H₁₀N₂O₄"],0,
 "The COOH of glycine condenses with the NH₂ of alanine, releasing one H₂O and forming one amide (peptide) bond, –C(=O)–NH–. Formula: C₂H₅NO₂ + C₃H₇NO₂ − H₂O = C₅H₁₀N₂O₃ (146.15 g/mol). C₅H₁₂N₂O₄ forgets the lost water; an ester link would need an O–H, not an N–H, partner; two amino acids form only one peptide bond (two would release two waters, C₅H₈N₂O₂); C₅H₁₀N₂O₄ removes H₂ instead of H₂O.",
 ["Which group of one amino acid reacts with which group of the other?","A small molecule is lost when the link forms.","Add the two formulas and subtract H₂O."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q30"}],
["m11-p-006",11,23,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which condensed structure is a dipeptide?",
 ["H₂N–CH₂–C(=O)–O–CH₂–COOH","H₂N–CH₂–CH₂–NH–CH₂–COOH","H₂N–CH(CH₃)–COOH","H₂N–CH₂–C(=O)–NH–CH(CH₃)–C(=O)–NH–CH₂–COOH","H₂N–CH₂–C(=O)–NH–CH(CH₃)–COOH"],4,
 "A dipeptide is two amino acids joined by ONE peptide (amide) bond, –C(=O)–NH–, with a free H₂N– at one end and a free –COOH at the other: H₂N–CH₂–C(=O)–NH–CH(CH₃)–COOH (Gly-Ala). The –C(=O)–O– compound is an ester; the –CH₂–NH–CH₂– compound is an amine with no C=O next to the nitrogen; H₂N–CH(CH₃)–COOH is a single amino acid (alanine); the chain with two –C(=O)–NH– links is a tripeptide.",
 ["Look for the amide link –C(=O)–NH–.","Count how many such links each structure has.","Exactly one link, with free NH₂ and COOH ends."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q30"}],
["m11-p-007",11,23,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "A pentapeptide is a chain of five amino acids. How many peptide bonds does it contain, and how many water molecules were released when it formed?",
 ["4 peptide bonds; 4 water molecules","5 peptide bonds; 5 water molecules","4 peptide bonds; 5 water molecules","5 peptide bonds; 4 water molecules","10 peptide bonds; 4 water molecules"],0,
 "A chain of n units has n − 1 links: 5 amino acids → 4 peptide bonds. Each peptide bond forms by condensation, releasing one H₂O, so 4 waters were released. Counting the amino acids themselves (5) instead of the links between them is the usual slip.",
 ["Picture five beads on a string - how many connections?","Each connection is one peptide bond.","Each peptide bond releases one water."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q29"}],
["m11-p-008",11,23,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "A protein folds in water. Which amino acid's side chain would you expect to point outward, into the water? (Side chains in parentheses.)",
 ["Valine (–CH(CH₃)₂)","Glutamine (–CH₂CH₂C(=O)NH₂)","Phenylalanine (–CH₂C₆H₅)","Isoleucine (–CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃)","Methionine (–CH₂CH₂SCH₃)"],1,
 "In water a protein folds so that nonpolar (hydrophobic) side chains are buried inside and polar or charged side chains face the water, where they can hydrogen bond with it. Glutamine's amide side chain (C=O and N–H) is polar and hydrogen bonds with water. Valine, isoleucine and phenylalanine have hydrocarbon side chains, and methionine's C–S–C side chain is essentially nonpolar - all of them prefer the interior.",
 ["Like dissolves like: what faces the water?","Look for a side chain with O–H, N–H or a charge.","Hydrocarbon side chains hide inside."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q31"}],
["m11-p-009",11,24,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Compared with pure water at the same temperature and pressure, how does a 1.0 m aqueous CaCl₂ solution behave?",
 ["Higher boiling point, lower freezing point, lower vapor pressure","Higher boiling point, higher freezing point, higher vapor pressure","Lower boiling point, lower freezing point, lower vapor pressure","Higher boiling point, lower freezing point, higher vapor pressure","The same boiling point, freezing point and vapor pressure, since CaCl₂ is nonvolatile"],0,
 "A nonvolatile solute lowers the solvent's vapor pressure (Raoult's law). With a lower vapor pressure, a higher temperature is needed to reach 1 atm, so the boiling point is elevated. The solute also disrupts freezing, so the freezing point is depressed. With i = 3 for CaCl₂, every effect is three times that of 1.0 m sugar. Being nonvolatile is exactly WHY these effects happen.",
 ["Colligative properties: what does a dissolved solute do to the vapor pressure?","Lower vapor pressure → boiling needs a higher temperature.","Boiling point up, freezing point down."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q33"}],
["m11-p-010",11,24,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "A glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) syrup in water contains 30.0 g of glucose in every 100.0 g of syrup; its density is 1.12 g/mL. Find the molality of glucose.",
 ["1.67 m","1.86 m","2.38 m","2.66 m","3.89 m"],2,
 "Take 100.0 g of solution: 30.0 g glucose and 70.0 g = 0.0700 kg water. mol glucose = 30.0 ÷ 180.16 = 0.1665 mol. Molality = 0.1665 mol ÷ 0.0700 kg = 2.38 m. The density is not needed for molality (it would be for molarity). 1.67 m divides by the kg of SOLUTION; 1.86 is the molarity (using 89.3 mL of solution); 2.66 m multiplies by the density; 3.89 is the moles of water in the sample (70.0 ÷ 18.02).",
 ["Assume 100.0 g of solution.","Molality = mol solute ÷ kg of SOLVENT.","0.1665 mol ÷ 0.0700 kg."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q34"}],
["m11-p-011",11,24,4,"Exam","num",
 "An aqueous NaCl solution has a molality of 2.50 m. What is its mass percent of NaCl? Enter a number in percent.",
 ["12.7%"],12.75,
 "2.50 m means 2.50 mol of NaCl per 1000 g of water. Mass of NaCl = 2.50 × 58.44 = 146.1 g. Mass of solution = 1000 + 146.1 = 1146.1 g. Mass % = 146.1 ÷ 1146.1 × 100 = 12.7%. Dividing by the 1000 g of water alone gives 14.6% - mass percent is per mass of SOLUTION.",
 ["Use 1000 g (1 kg) of water as your basis.","Convert 2.50 mol of NaCl to grams.","Divide by water + NaCl, not by the water alone."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q34"}],
["m11-p-012",11,24,3,"Trap","mcq",
 "Each solution below is aqueous. Which one boils at the highest temperature?",
 ["1.0 m glucose","0.40 m MgCl₂","0.55 m NaNO₃","0.45 m K₂SO₄","Pure water"],3,
 "ΔT(b) = i·K(b)·m, so compare i × m (moles of particles per kg): glucose 1 × 1.0 = 1.0; MgCl₂ 3 × 0.40 = 1.20; NaNO₃ 2 × 0.55 = 1.10; K₂SO₄ 3 × 0.45 = 1.35 (2 K⁺ + 1 SO₄²⁻). K₂SO₄ has the most dissolved particles → highest boiling point. Glucose has the largest molality but does not dissociate (i = 1); pure water boils lowest of all.",
 ["Boiling-point elevation depends on the number of dissolved particles.","Multiply the molality by the van 't Hoff factor i.","K₂SO₄ → 3 ions; MgCl₂ → 3 ions; NaNO₃ → 2."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q35"}],
["m11-p-013",11,24,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which aqueous solution has the LOWEST freezing point?",
 ["0.35 m CaCl₂","0.50 m NaCl","1.10 m ethylene glycol","0.25 m Na₃PO₄","0.30 m Al(NO₃)₃"],4,
 "The lowest freezing point has the largest ΔT(f), i.e. the largest i × m. CaCl₂ 3 × 0.35 = 1.05; NaCl 2 × 0.50 = 1.00; ethylene glycol (a nonelectrolyte) 1 × 1.10 = 1.10; Na₃PO₄ 4 × 0.25 = 1.00; Al(NO₃)₃ 4 × 0.30 = 1.20 (Al³⁺ + 3 NO₃⁻). Polyatomic ions stay intact - Al(NO₃)₃ gives 4 particles, not 13.",
 ["Freezing-point depression grows with i × m.","Find i for each, keeping polyatomic ions whole.","Compare 1.05, 1.00, 1.10, 1.00 and 1.20."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q35"}],
["m11-p-014",11,24,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "A lab wants water that stays liquid down to −5.0 °C. How many grams of glycerol, C₃H₈O₃ (a nonelectrolyte, 92.09 g/mol), should be dissolved in 2.00 kg of water? (K(f) = 1.86 °C/m)",
 ["124 g","248 g","495 g","1.71 × 10³ g","1.80 × 10³ g"],2,
 "ΔT(f) = 5.0 °C = K(f)·m → m = 5.0 ÷ 1.86 = 2.69 mol/kg. Moles = 2.69 mol/kg × 2.00 kg = 5.38 mol. Mass = 5.38 × 92.09 = 495 g. 248 g treats the 2.00 kg as 1 kg; 124 g divides by 2.00 kg instead of multiplying; 1.71 × 10³ g multiplies ΔT by K(f); 1.80 × 10³ g uses water's K(b) (0.512) instead of K(f).",
 ["ΔT(f) = i·K(f)·m with i = 1.","molality × kg of water = moles of glycerol.","2.69 m × 2.00 kg × 92.09 g/mol."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q33"}],
["m11-p-015",11,24,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "How many grams of CaCl₂ (110.98 g/mol; assume complete dissociation, i = 3) dissolved in 500. g of water give a solution that freezes at −3.00 °C? (K(f) = 1.86 °C/m)",
 ["29.8 g","44.8 g","59.7 g","89.5 g","108 g"],0,
 "m = ΔT(f) ÷ (i·K(f)) = 3.00 ÷ (3 × 1.86) = 0.538 mol/kg. Moles of CaCl₂ = 0.538 × 0.500 kg = 0.269 mol. Mass = 0.269 × 110.98 = 29.8 g. 89.5 g ignores i (as if CaCl₂ did not dissociate); 44.8 g uses i = 2; 59.7 g uses 1 kg of water; 108 g uses K(b) instead of K(f).",
 ["Include the van 't Hoff factor: CaCl₂ → 3 ions.","Find the molality, then multiply by 0.500 kg.","0.538 m × 0.500 kg × 110.98 g/mol."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q33"}],
["m11-p-016",11,24,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "A red blood cell that was sitting in an isotonic medium is transferred to 3% NaCl, a solution far more concentrated in solute than the cell's interior. What happens to the cell?",
 ["Water moves into the cell, which swells and may burst","Water moves out of the cell, which shrinks (crenates)","Salt moves into the cell until it bursts","Nothing - osmosis only happens with pure water","The cell's DNA replication speeds up"],1,
 "Across a semipermeable membrane water flows from the region of LOWER solute concentration to HIGHER. The 3% NaCl outside is hypertonic, so water leaves the cell and it shrivels (crenation). Swelling and bursting (hemolysis) happens in pure water or a hypotonic solution - the opposite case. The membrane passes water, not the salt, and osmosis happens whenever the concentrations differ.",
 ["Which way does water flow during osmosis?","Compare the solute concentrations inside and outside the cell.","Water moves toward the more concentrated side."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q36"}],

/* ---------- Gym 13: nuclear chemistry ---------- */
["m13-p-001",13,27,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Starting from ²³⁸₉₂U, which sequence of emissions ends at ²³⁰₉₀Th?",
 ["α, α","α, α, β⁻","α, β⁻, β⁻","α, β⁻, β⁻, α","α, β⁺, β⁺, α"],3,
 "Mass number: 238 → 230 is a loss of 8, so exactly two α particles (β particles do not change A). Atomic number: two α take Z from 92 to 88, but thorium is 90, so two β⁻ decays must each add 1: 88 + 2 = 90. α, β⁻, β⁻, α does that (²³⁸₉₂U → ²³⁴₉₀Th → ²³⁴₉₁Pa → ²³⁴₉₂U → ²³⁰₉₀Th). α, α gives ²³⁰Ra (Z = 88); α, α, β⁻ gives ²³⁰Ac (Z = 89); α, β⁻, β⁻ gives ²³⁴U; β⁺ lowers Z further, to 86 (Rn).",
 ["Fix the mass number first: only α changes A (by 4).","Then fix Z: α −2, β⁻ +1, β⁺ −1.","Two α and two β⁻."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q1"}],
["m13-p-002",13,27,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Argon-40 (Z = 18) is common in Earth's atmosphere. Which of these processes would yield it?",
 ["Positron emission by calcium-40 (⁴⁰Ca)","Beta (β⁻) decay of potassium-40 (⁴⁰K)","Alpha decay of argon-44 (⁴⁴Ar)","Gamma emission by argon-41 (⁴¹Ar)","Electron capture by potassium-40 (⁴⁰K)"],4,
 "Electron capture turns a proton into a neutron: A stays 40 and Z drops from 19 (K) to 18 (Ar). ⁴⁰K β⁻ decay goes the other way, to ⁴⁰Ca (Z = 20). Positron emission by ⁴⁰Ca gives ⁴⁰K (Z = 19). Alpha decay of ⁴⁴Ar gives ⁴⁰S (Z = 16). Gamma emission changes neither A nor Z, so ⁴¹Ar stays ⁴¹Ar.",
 ["Write each process as a change in (A, Z).","You need A = 40 and Z = 18 at the end.","Which process lowers Z by one and keeps A?"],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q2"}],
["m13-p-003",13,27,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which pair of processes could EACH convert iron-55 (⁵⁵₂₆Fe) into manganese-55 (⁵⁵₂₅Mn)?",
 ["β⁻ emission or electron capture","α emission or β⁻ emission","Positron emission or β⁻ emission","Gamma emission or electron capture","Positron emission or electron capture"],4,
 "⁵⁵Fe → ⁵⁵Mn keeps A = 55 and lowers Z by 1. Positron emission (a proton becomes a neutron and a β⁺ is emitted) and electron capture (a proton captures an inner electron and becomes a neutron) both do exactly that. β⁻ emission RAISES Z by 1 (it would give ⁵⁵Co); α emission lowers A by 4; gamma emission changes nothing.",
 ["What change in A and Z takes ⁵⁵Fe to ⁵⁵Mn?","List every process that lowers Z by one without changing A.","There are two."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q2"}],
["m13-p-004",13,28,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Phosphorus-32 has a half-life of 14.3 days. A sample initially contains 2.40 mg of ³²P. What mass of ³²P is left after 57.2 days?",
 ["0.150 mg","0 mg","0.0750 mg","0.300 mg","0.600 mg"],0,
 "57.2 days ÷ 14.3 days = 4.00 half-lives. Remaining = 2.40 mg × (½)⁴ = 2.40 ÷ 16 = 0.150 mg. 0.600 mg stops after two halvings and 0.300 mg after three; 0.0750 mg halves five times; radioactive decay halves the amount each half-life and never simply runs out.",
 ["How many half-lives fit in 57.2 days?","Each half-life halves the amount.","2.40 mg × (½)⁴."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q3"}],
["m13-p-005",13,28,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "In the fusion of two deuterium nuclei into helium, how does the total mass of the products compare with the total mass of the reactants?",
 ["The products have slightly less mass; the difference is released as energy","The products have slightly more mass","The products have exactly the same mass, since mass is conserved","The products have about half the mass","The products have about twice the mass"],0,
 "In both fusion and fission the products are more tightly bound than the reactants, so a little mass disappears and appears as energy: ΔE = Δm·c². Because c² is enormous, a tiny loss of mass releases a huge amount of energy. Mass is conserved in chemical reactions (to within measurement) but not in nuclear reactions. A change of half or double would be absurdly large.",
 ["Fusion releases energy. Where does that energy come from?","Einstein: E = mc².","Energy released means mass lost."],{src:"2017 FALL Final Q4"}],
["m13-p-006",13,28,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Iodine-131, used to treat thyroid disease, has a half-life of 8.02 days. After how many days will a dose's activity have dropped to 12.0% of its initial value?",
 ["1.48 days","17.0 days","24.5 days","66.8 days","8.02 days"],2,
 "First-order decay: k = 0.693 ÷ 8.02 d = 0.0864 d⁻¹. ln(N₀/N) = kt → t = ln(1/0.120) ÷ 0.0864 d⁻¹ = 2.120 ÷ 0.0864 = 24.5 days (about 3.06 half-lives - a little past ⅛ = 12.5%). 1.48 days uses the 88.0% that decayed instead of the 12.0% that remains; 17.0 days forgets to divide by ln 2; 66.8 days divides t½ by 0.120; 8.02 days is a single half-life (50% left).",
 ["k = 0.693 / t½.","ln(N₀/N) = kt, with N/N₀ = 0.120 (the fraction REMAINING).","12.5% would be exactly 3 half-lives; 12.0% takes a little longer."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q2"}],
["m13-p-007",13,27,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "In 1919 Rutherford bombarded nitrogen-14 with alpha particles: ¹⁴₇N + ⁴₂He → ¹⁷₈O + X. What is X?",
 ["A proton","A neutron","An alpha particle","A β⁻ particle","A positron"],0,
 "Balance the mass numbers: 14 + 4 = 17 + A → A = 1. Balance the atomic numbers: 7 + 2 = 8 + Z → Z = 1. A particle with A = 1 and Z = 1 is a proton, ¹₁H. A neutron has Z = 0; a β⁻ particle has A = 0, Z = −1; a positron has A = 0, Z = +1.",
 ["The top numbers (A) must total the same on both sides.","So must the bottom numbers (Z).","A = 1, Z = 1."],{src:"2018 FALL Final Q3"}],
["m13-p-008",13,27,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Carbon-11 (6 protons, 5 neutrons) is a short-lived tracer; the only stable carbon nuclides are ¹²C and ¹³C. By which mode would you predict ¹¹C decays?",
 ["Positron emission","β⁻ decay","Alpha decay","Gamma emission only","Spontaneous fission"],0,
 "¹¹C has 6 protons and only 5 neutrons - fewer neutrons than the stable isotopes, so it lies BELOW the band of stability (too many protons). It moves toward stability by turning a proton into a neutron: positron emission (or electron capture), giving ¹¹B. β⁻ decay is for neutron-rich nuclei (here it would make N/Z worse); alpha decay and spontaneous fission belong to very heavy nuclei; gamma emission alone does not change N or Z.",
 ["Compare ¹¹C's neutron count with that of stable ¹²C and ¹³C.","Too few neutrons → convert a proton into a neutron.","Which listed mode does p → n?"],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q16"}],
["m13-p-009",13,27,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "A nucleus emits a positron. How do its mass number (A) and atomic number (Z) change?",
 ["Mass number unchanged; atomic number increases by 1","Mass number decreases by 4; atomic number decreases by 2","Mass number unchanged; atomic number decreases by 1","Mass number decreases by 1; atomic number unchanged","Neither changes"],2,
 "A positron is ⁰₊₁β (mass number 0, charge +1). Balancing the nuclear equation, the product keeps the same A and has Z − 1: a proton has become a neutron. Z + 1 is β⁻ decay; A − 4 and Z − 2 is alpha decay; no change at all is gamma emission.",
 ["Write the positron's symbol with its A and Z.","A positron has A = 0 and charge +1.","Subtract: the product keeps A and loses 1 from Z."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q17"}],
["m13-p-010",13,27,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Thorium-232 (²³²₉₀Th) decays through five steps: α, then β⁻, then β⁻, then α, then α. What nuclide is formed after the fifth step?",
 ["²²⁰₈₄Po","²²⁴₈₈Ra","²²⁸₉₀Th","²²⁰₈₅At","²²⁰₈₆Rn"],4,
 "Track (A, Z): ²³²₉₀Th → α → ²²⁸₈₈Ra → β⁻ → ²²⁸₈₉Ac → β⁻ → ²²⁸₉₀Th → α → ²²⁴₈₈Ra → α → ²²⁰₈₆Rn. Overall: three α (A − 12, Z − 6) and two β⁻ (Z + 2) give A = 220, Z = 86, radon. ²²⁰Po ignores both β⁻ steps; ²²⁰At counts only one; ²²⁴Ra stops after four steps; ²²⁸Th is the nuclide after three steps.",
 ["α: A − 4, Z − 2. β⁻: A unchanged, Z + 1.","Total change: three α and two β⁻.","A = 232 − 12; Z = 90 − 6 + 2."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q18"}],
["m13-p-011",13,28,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "One fission pathway of uranium-235 is ²³⁵₉₂U + ¹₀n → ¹³⁹₅₆Ba + 3 ¹₀n + X. What is X?",
 ["⁹⁴₃₆Kr","⁹⁶₃₆Kr","⁹³₃₆Kr","⁹⁴₃₈Sr","⁹⁷₃₆Kr"],0,
 "Mass numbers: 235 + 1 = 139 + 3(1) + A → A = 94. Atomic numbers: 92 + 0 = 56 + 3(0) + Z → Z = 36 (krypton). ⁹³Kr forgets the incoming neutron; ⁹⁶Kr counts only one outgoing neutron; ⁹⁷Kr ignores all three outgoing neutrons; ⁹⁴Sr has the right A but Z = 38 does not balance 92 − 56.",
 ["Count every neutron, including the one that is absorbed.","Left side: A = 236 and Z = 92.","A = 236 − 139 − 3; Z = 92 − 56."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q19"}],
["m13-p-012",13,27,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Oxygen-15 (¹⁵₈O) is a positron emitter used in PET scans. What nuclide does it become?",
 ["¹⁵₉F","¹⁴₇N","¹¹₆C","¹⁶₈O","¹⁵₇N"],4,
 "Positron emission: ¹⁵₈O → ⁰₊₁β + ¹⁵₇N. A stays 15 and Z drops by 1 to 7, nitrogen. ¹⁵F raises Z (that would be β⁻ decay); ¹⁴N loses a mass unit as if a proton had been emitted; ¹⁶O gains a neutron; ¹¹C would be alpha decay.",
 ["Positron emission: A unchanged, Z − 1.","Which element has Z = 7?","Keep the mass number 15."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q20"}],
["m13-p-013",13,28,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Sodium-24 has a half-life of 15.0 hours. A sample starts with exactly 8000 ²⁴Na atoms. How many ²⁴Na atoms remain after 3.75 days?",
 ["0","62","1333","250","125"],4,
 "3.75 days × 24 h/day = 90.0 h, and 90.0 h ÷ 15.0 h = 6 half-lives. Remaining = 8000 × (½)⁶ = 8000 ÷ 64 = 125 atoms. 250 stops after five halvings; 62 halves seven times; 1333 divides by 6 instead of by 2⁶; the sample does not simply run out after a few half-lives.",
 ["Convert days to hours first.","Number of half-lives = 90.0 h ÷ 15.0 h.","8000 × (½)⁶."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q21"}],
["m13-p-014",13,28,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "A detector counts 2.43 × 10¹⁵ β⁻ particles leaving a 2.00 × 10⁻⁶ mol sample of phosphorus-32 over one hour. Assuming the rate stays constant during that hour, find the half-life of ³²P in hours.",
 ["2.02 × 10⁻³ h","1.40 × 10⁻³ h","344 h","496 h","5.70 × 10⁻²² h"],2,
 "Number of nuclei: N = 2.00 × 10⁻⁶ mol × 6.022 × 10²³ = 1.204 × 10¹⁸. Rate = kN → k = 2.43 × 10¹⁵ h⁻¹ ÷ 1.204 × 10¹⁸ = 2.018 × 10⁻³ h⁻¹. t½ = 0.693 ÷ k = 344 h (14.3 days). 2.02 × 10⁻³ h reports k itself; 496 h is 1/k (forgets ln 2); 1.40 × 10⁻³ h multiplies k by 0.693; 5.70 × 10⁻²² h uses moles instead of nuclei for N.",
 ["Decay rate = k × (number of nuclei).","Convert moles to nuclei with Avogadro's number.","t½ = 0.693/k."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q22"}],
["m13-p-015",13,28,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "A 1.00 μg sample of cobalt-60 (59.93 g/mol) has an activity of 4.19 × 10⁷ decays per second. What is the half-life of ⁶⁰Co in years? (1 yr = 3.156 × 10⁷ s)",
 ["7.60 yr","5.27 yr","126 yr","1.92 × 10³ yr","1.66 × 10⁸ yr"],1,
 "N = (1.00 × 10⁻⁶ g ÷ 59.93 g/mol) × 6.022 × 10²³ = 1.005 × 10¹⁶ nuclei. k = activity ÷ N = 4.19 × 10⁷ s⁻¹ ÷ 1.005 × 10¹⁶ = 4.17 × 10⁻⁹ s⁻¹. t½ = 0.693 ÷ k = 1.66 × 10⁸ s ÷ 3.156 × 10⁷ s/yr = 5.27 yr. 7.60 yr leaves out ln 2; 1.66 × 10⁸ is the half-life in seconds labelled as years; 1.92 × 10³ is the half-life in days; 126 yr divides by 3600 and by 365.25 but forgets the 24 hours in a day.",
 ["μg → g → mol → nuclei.","k = activity ÷ N, in s⁻¹.","t½ = 0.693/k, then convert seconds to years."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q22"}],
["m13-p-016",13,28,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Potassium-40 has a half-life of 1.25 × 10⁹ years. A rock has lost 87.5% of the ⁴⁰K it contained when it solidified. How old is the rock?",
 ["2.41 × 10⁸ years","1.09 × 10⁹ years","2.50 × 10⁹ years","3.75 × 10⁹ years","1.09 × 10¹⁰ years"],3,
 "87.5% decayed means 12.5% = ⅛ remains. ⅛ = (½)³, so 3 half-lives: 3 × 1.25 × 10⁹ = 3.75 × 10⁹ years. 2.41 × 10⁸ treats 87.5% as the fraction remaining; 1.09 × 10⁹ multiplies t½ by 0.875; 2.50 × 10⁹ is two half-lives (25% left); 1.09 × 10¹⁰ multiplies t½ by 8.75.",
 ["Turn 'lost 87.5%' into the fraction remaining.","12.5% = 1/8 = (1/2)ⁿ.","n × 1.25 × 10⁹ years."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q23"}],
["m13-p-017",13,28,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "A lump of uranium-235 smaller than the critical mass cannot sustain a fission chain reaction. Why?",
 ["Uranium-235 stops releasing neutrons when the lump is small","Too many of the released neutrons escape through the surface instead of striking other ²³⁵U nuclei","Small lumps contain only uranium-238","Fission can only begin after fusion has heated the lump","A small lump has too high a mass number to split"],1,
 "Each fission releases 2-3 neutrons, and a chain reaction continues only if, on average, at least one of them causes another fission. In a small lump a large fraction of the neutrons reach the surface and escape. Above the critical mass enough neutrons are captured inside to keep the chain going. Every ²³⁵U fission still releases neutrons, whatever the size of the lump.",
 ["What carries a chain reaction from one nucleus to the next?","Think about surface area compared with volume.","Escaping neutrons cannot cause new fissions."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q24"}],
["m13-p-018",13,28,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "An imaginary element, Vb, captures a neutron and splits: Vb + ¹₀n → ¹³⁸₅₄Xe + ¹¹⁰₄₆Pd + 2 ¹₀n. What are the atomic number and mass number of Vb?",
 ["Z = 100, A = 249","Z = 100, A = 250","Z = 100, A = 248","Z = 98, A = 249","Z = 102, A = 251"],0,
 "Mass numbers: A + 1 = 138 + 110 + 2(1) = 250 → A = 249. Atomic numbers: Z + 0 = 54 + 46 + 2(0) → Z = 100. A = 250 forgets to subtract the captured neutron; A = 248 subtracts a neutron too many; Z = 98 and Z = 102 treat the neutrons as if they changed the atomic number.",
 ["Neutrons have A = 1 and Z = 0.","Add up the products' A values, then remove the captured neutron.","Z = 54 + 46."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q25"}],
["m13-p-019",13,27,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "A parent nuclide undergoes β⁻ decay and the daughter is sulfur-32 (³²₁₆S). Identify the parent.",
 ["³²₁₇Cl","³⁶₁₈Ar","³²₁₅P","³³₁₅P","³¹₁₅P"],2,
 "β⁻ decay keeps A and raises Z by 1 (a neutron becomes a proton). Working backwards, the parent has A = 32 and Z = 16 − 1 = 15: ³²₁₅P. ³²Cl would give ³²S by POSITRON emission, and ³⁶Ar would give it by ALPHA decay - right product, wrong process. ³³P and ³¹P have the wrong mass number.",
 ["Write the β⁻ change: A unchanged, Z + 1.","Run it backwards from ³²₁₆S.","The parent has A = 32 and Z = 15."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q26"}],
["m13-p-020",13,27,2,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which of these nuclides would you expect to be non-radioactive?",
 ["¹¹₃Li","¹⁶₁₀Ne","⁴⁰₂₀Ca","³⁴₁₃Al","²⁶²₁₀₆Sg"],2,
 "⁴⁰Ca has 20 protons and 20 neutrons: N/Z = 1 (right on the band for a light nucleus), both numbers even, and both equal to the magic number 20. ¹¹Li (N/Z = 2.7) and ³⁴Al (N/Z = 1.6, far above 1 for so light a nucleus) have too many neutrons; ¹⁶Ne (N/Z = 0.6) has far too many protons; Sg (Z = 106) lies beyond bismuth (Z = 83), where every nucleus is radioactive.",
 ["For light nuclei the stable N/Z ratio is about 1.","Magic numbers: 2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82.","No element beyond Z = 83 has a stable isotope."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q27"}],
["m13-p-021",13,27,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "²³⁴₉₂U first emits an α particle. The daughter nucleus is itself unstable and has two possible decay routes, α or β⁻. Which pair lists the two possible granddaughter nuclides?",
 ["²²⁶₈₈Ra and ²³⁰₉₁Pa","²²⁶₈₈Ra and ²³⁰₉₀Th","²³⁰₉₁Pa and ²³⁰₉₂U","²²⁶₈₉Ac and ²³⁰₉₁Pa","²²⁶₈₈Ra and ²³⁰₈₉Ac"],0,
 "Step 1 (α): ²³⁴₉₂U → ²³⁰₉₀Th. Step 2 from ²³⁰Th: α gives ²²⁶₈₈Ra; β⁻ gives ²³⁰₉₁Pa. ²³⁰Th is only the product of the first step; β⁻ RAISES Z, so it cannot give ²³⁰Ac (Z = 89); ²²⁶Ac has the wrong Z for an α product; ²³⁰U would need two β⁻ steps after the α.",
 ["Do the first α step: A − 4, Z − 2.","From that nuclide, branch: α or β⁻.","β⁻ increases Z by one."],{src:"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q28"}]
]);
